INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$54c6128b$SATQ$, $SATQ$f1173373-68e6-4e0b-bb17-9e691425cb0e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f55bb608-2044-436b-99a8-0e60508bc8d2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>When ancient oak planks were unearthed during subway construction in Rome, Mauro Bernabei and his team examined the growth rings in the wood to determine where these planks came from. By comparing the growth rings on the planks to records of similar rings in oaks from Europe, the team could trace the wood to the Jura region of France, hundreds of kilometers from Rome. <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>Because timber could only have been transported from distant Jura to Rome by boat, the team&rsquo;s findings suggest the complexity of Roman trade routes.</u></span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"14ba9f0c-1598-46ee-a3de-298c14972bbe","content":"<p>It presents a conclusion about Roman trade routes based on the team&rsquo;s findings.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"4199198e-f667-48ab-ab38-f5e2cf5c4199","content":"<p>It questions how the team was able to conclude that the planks were used to build a boat.</p>"},{"id":"7340023c-e20b-491b-b745-872460fa0bd1","content":"<p>It explains why the planks were made from oak rather than a different kind of wood.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"a0a26c3e-58c9-4927-b39f-a0ec0586e5dd","content":"<p>It describes common methods used in Roman subway construction.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["14ba9f0c-1598-46ee-a3de-298c14972bbe"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The first sentence explains that Bernabei and his team studied growth rings to obtain information about the ancient oak planks found during a construction project in Rome. The next sentence presents what the researchers learned: the wood from the planks came from France&rsquo;s Jura region, which is far from Rome. The underlined sentence then presents the implications of the findings about the planks: the wood must have been brought to Rome by boat, a difficult task that suggests Roman trade routes were complex. Thus, the underlined sentence mainly functions to present a conclusion about Roman trade routes based on the team&rsquo;s findings. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the team thought the ancient planks were used in the construction of a boat, nor does the underlined sentence question that conclusion. Instead, the text states that the wood could only have been transported from Jura to Rome in a boat. Choice C is incorrect because the underlined sentence simply offers a conclusion drawn from the team&rsquo;s findings about the likely place of origin of the ancient planks; the text never mentions why oak was chosen for the planks instead of other wood. Choice D is incorrect because neither the underlined sentence nor the text as a whole addresses any methods that Romans used in constructing subways. Instead, the underlined sentence offers a conclusion drawn from the team&rsquo;s findings about the likely place of origin of the ancient wooden planks discovered. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-046-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$4324799f-4d41-4b31-a90f-821f59bbd263$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"54c6128b","external_id":"f1173373-68e6-4e0b-bb17-9e691425cb0e","vaultid":"f55bb608-2044-436b-99a8-0e60508bc8d2","uId":"ab1942cc-c8a3-4152-89f3-830501d63d60","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>When ancient oak planks were unearthed during subway construction in Rome, Mauro Bernabei and his team examined the growth rings in the wood to determine where these planks came from. By comparing the growth rings on the planks to records of similar rings in oaks from Europe, the team could trace the wood to the Jura region of France, hundreds of kilometers from Rome. <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>Because timber could only have been transported from distant Jura to Rome by boat, the team&rsquo;s findings suggest the complexity of Roman trade routes.</u></span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"14ba9f0c-1598-46ee-a3de-298c14972bbe","content":"<p>It presents a conclusion about Roman trade routes based on the team&rsquo;s findings.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"4199198e-f667-48ab-ab38-f5e2cf5c4199","content":"<p>It questions how the team was able to conclude that the planks were used to build a boat.</p>"},{"id":"7340023c-e20b-491b-b745-872460fa0bd1","content":"<p>It explains why the planks were made from oak rather than a different kind of wood.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"a0a26c3e-58c9-4927-b39f-a0ec0586e5dd","content":"<p>It describes common methods used in Roman subway construction.</p>"}],"keys":["14ba9f0c-1598-46ee-a3de-298c14972bbe"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The first sentence explains that Bernabei and his team studied growth rings to obtain information about the ancient oak planks found during a construction project in Rome. The next sentence presents what the researchers learned: the wood from the planks came from France&rsquo;s Jura region, which is far from Rome. The underlined sentence then presents the implications of the findings about the planks: the wood must have been brought to Rome by boat, a difficult task that suggests Roman trade routes were complex. Thus, the underlined sentence mainly functions to present a conclusion about Roman trade routes based on the team&rsquo;s findings. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the team thought the ancient planks were used in the construction of a boat, nor does the underlined sentence question that conclusion. Instead, the text states that the wood could only have been transported from Jura to Rome in a boat. Choice C is incorrect because the underlined sentence simply offers a conclusion drawn from the team&rsquo;s findings about the likely place of origin of the ancient planks; the text never mentions why oak was chosen for the planks instead of other wood. Choice D is incorrect because neither the underlined sentence nor the text as a whole addresses any methods that Romans used in constructing subways. Instead, the underlined sentence offers a conclusion drawn from the team&rsquo;s findings about the likely place of origin of the ancient wooden planks discovered. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-046-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"4324799f-4d41-4b31-a90f-821f59bbd263","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959323,"updateDate":1691007959323}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959323, 1691007959323),
    ($SATQ$5514fd1f$SATQ$, $SATQ$a31bc539-61e1-4832-8a73-3c1d92e98529$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Archaeologist Erika Karuzas works with the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes of Montana to preserve culturally significant sites. In 2015 they decided that creating a new national trail connecting many of these sites would <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> their preservation efforts, because the US government provides some protections to national trails that aren&rsquo;t given to other areas.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"30e1e8c9-b7a1-4ac0-ac58-2bf4b1af4a3f","content":"<p>inspire</p>"},{"id":"1808db8b-28a2-4846-903e-7b4a0084cb7b","content":"<p>ignore</p>"},{"id":"fa8d0a1d-f5a4-4b7e-b196-135e32178257","content":"<p>promote</p>"},{"id":"397cd3a8-9737-4542-827b-9acb089bcc1b","content":"<p>reduce</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["fa8d0a1d-f5a4-4b7e-b196-135e32178257"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the preservation efforts undertaken by Erika Karuzas and the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes of Montana. In this context, "promote" means play a part in the success or growth of something or someone. The text indicates that Karuzas and the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes work to preserve culturally important sites. It goes on to say that their decision to create a national trail connecting the sites was motivated by the fact that such a trail would receive protections from the US government that the sites wouldn&rsquo;t otherwise receive. Thus, such a decision would promote the preservation efforts.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "inspire" means motivate or influence, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text establishes that Karuzas and the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes of Montana work to preserve culturally significant sites and that their decision to create a new national trail occurred as a result of a desire to add protections to the sites that they wouldn&rsquo;t otherwise receive. Thus, creating the trail wouldn&rsquo;t inspire the efforts to preserve the sites; instead, it would promote the efforts. Choice B is incorrect because "ignore" means overlook, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text indicates that Karuzas and the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes of Montana work to preserve culturally significant sites and that they decided to create a national trail connecting the sites because such a trail would receive protections from the US government that the sites wouldn&rsquo;t otherwise receive. Thus, the creation of the trail would promote, not ignore, the preservation efforts. Choice D is incorrect because "reduce" means decrease or restrict, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text indicates that Karuzas and the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes of Montana work to preserve culturally significant sites and that they decided to create a national trail connecting the sites because such a trail would receive protections from the US government that the sites wouldn&rsquo;t otherwise receive. The decision would therefore promote, not reduce, the preservation efforts.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5514fd1f","external_id":"a31bc539-61e1-4832-8a73-3c1d92e98529","vaultid":null,"uId":"59208895-3420-46ba-aac3-71970c06b4ee","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Archaeologist Erika Karuzas works with the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes of Montana to preserve culturally significant sites. In 2015 they decided that creating a new national trail connecting many of these sites would <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> their preservation efforts, because the US government provides some protections to national trails that aren&rsquo;t given to other areas.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"30e1e8c9-b7a1-4ac0-ac58-2bf4b1af4a3f","content":"<p>inspire</p>"},{"id":"1808db8b-28a2-4846-903e-7b4a0084cb7b","content":"<p>ignore</p>"},{"id":"fa8d0a1d-f5a4-4b7e-b196-135e32178257","content":"<p>promote</p>"},{"id":"397cd3a8-9737-4542-827b-9acb089bcc1b","content":"<p>reduce</p>"}],"keys":["fa8d0a1d-f5a4-4b7e-b196-135e32178257"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the preservation efforts undertaken by Erika Karuzas and the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes of Montana. In this context, \"promote\" means play a part in the success or growth of something or someone. The text indicates that Karuzas and the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes work to preserve culturally important sites. It goes on to say that their decision to create a national trail connecting the sites was motivated by the fact that such a trail would receive protections from the US government that the sites wouldn&rsquo;t otherwise receive. Thus, such a decision would promote the preservation efforts.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"inspire\" means motivate or influence, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text establishes that Karuzas and the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes of Montana work to preserve culturally significant sites and that their decision to create a new national trail occurred as a result of a desire to add protections to the sites that they wouldn&rsquo;t otherwise receive. Thus, creating the trail wouldn&rsquo;t inspire the efforts to preserve the sites; instead, it would promote the efforts. Choice B is incorrect because \"ignore\" means overlook, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text indicates that Karuzas and the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes of Montana work to preserve culturally significant sites and that they decided to create a national trail connecting the sites because such a trail would receive protections from the US government that the sites wouldn&rsquo;t otherwise receive. Thus, the creation of the trail would promote, not ignore, the preservation efforts. Choice D is incorrect because \"reduce\" means decrease or restrict, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text indicates that Karuzas and the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes of Montana work to preserve culturally significant sites and that they decided to create a national trail connecting the sites because such a trail would receive protections from the US government that the sites wouldn&rsquo;t otherwise receive. The decision would therefore promote, not reduce, the preservation efforts.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323033,"updateDate":1730147323033}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323033, 1730147323033),
    ($SATQ$56ec23a0$SATQ$, $SATQ$cffc9b1f-c10b-4f3c-bde4-418ef75a5f08$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$a8a1dac2-5da0-41b1-858e-6e5f73eef2a3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Hiroshi Senju is known worldwide for his paintings of waterfalls. These paintings are large and tend not to show the entire waterfall.&nbsp; Instead, Senju focuses on just the point where the falling water reaches the pool below, keeping the top of the waterfall out of view. While Senju&rsquo;s paintings are rooted in art movements originating in the United States, the artist uses traditional Japanese techniques and materials that make his work instantly recognizable.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a649a554-522d-49ce-bce8-714e463d33db","content":"<p>It introduces an artist and then explains some common characteristics of well-known paintings by that artist.</p>"},{"id":"f863e1f6-857c-4639-9658-f0b3c454d024","content":"<p>It explains a specific painting technique and then provides examples of artists who use the technique.</p>"},{"id":"6dba2312-8046-408d-a37d-60e46e3e3163","content":"<p>It describes a famous painting and then compares it to a lesser-known painting from the same time period.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"4f2dbc38-9889-4ce9-b2b1-82b72d3bab67","content":"<p>It gives an opinion on an artist and then suggests multiple reasons why the artist&rsquo;s work has been largely overlooked.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a649a554-522d-49ce-bce8-714e463d33db"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The first sentence introduces Senju as a famous artist, while the next three sentences describe the defining features of his art, such as it only showing part of the waterfall and its origins in US art movements and Japanese techniques. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t provide examples of any other artists who use Senju&rsquo;s techniques. Choice C is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t describe any single famous painting or make comparisons between paintings. Choice D is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t provide an opinion on Senju (just facts), nor does it suggest that his art has been overlooked&mdash;in fact, it states that he is &ldquo;known worldwide.&rdquo; </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TSP-E006$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$bc1e8fb7-d52e-4ff6-8d6b-9b8048147147$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"56ec23a0","external_id":"cffc9b1f-c10b-4f3c-bde4-418ef75a5f08","vaultid":"a8a1dac2-5da0-41b1-858e-6e5f73eef2a3","uId":"c891e806-b841-42c9-a0c0-f2daa1bdf13d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Hiroshi Senju is known worldwide for his paintings of waterfalls. These paintings are large and tend not to show the entire waterfall.&nbsp; Instead, Senju focuses on just the point where the falling water reaches the pool below, keeping the top of the waterfall out of view. While Senju&rsquo;s paintings are rooted in art movements originating in the United States, the artist uses traditional Japanese techniques and materials that make his work instantly recognizable.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a649a554-522d-49ce-bce8-714e463d33db","content":"<p>It introduces an artist and then explains some common characteristics of well-known paintings by that artist.</p>"},{"id":"f863e1f6-857c-4639-9658-f0b3c454d024","content":"<p>It explains a specific painting technique and then provides examples of artists who use the technique.</p>"},{"id":"6dba2312-8046-408d-a37d-60e46e3e3163","content":"<p>It describes a famous painting and then compares it to a lesser-known painting from the same time period.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"4f2dbc38-9889-4ce9-b2b1-82b72d3bab67","content":"<p>It gives an opinion on an artist and then suggests multiple reasons why the artist&rsquo;s work has been largely overlooked.</p>"}],"keys":["a649a554-522d-49ce-bce8-714e463d33db"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The first sentence introduces Senju as a famous artist, while the next three sentences describe the defining features of his art, such as it only showing part of the waterfall and its origins in US art movements and Japanese techniques. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t provide examples of any other artists who use Senju&rsquo;s techniques. Choice C is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t describe any single famous painting or make comparisons between paintings. Choice D is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t provide an opinion on Senju (just facts), nor does it suggest that his art has been overlooked&mdash;in fact, it states that he is &ldquo;known worldwide.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TSP-E006","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"bc1e8fb7-d52e-4ff6-8d6b-9b8048147147","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959374,"updateDate":1691007959374}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959374, 1691007959374),
    ($SATQ$570970cd$SATQ$, $SATQ$bcba58ca-6a99-4a94-b120-d3a1909d3ad5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4d2acb3a-046f-43a0-a238-0d8adf9cffe8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from <em>Indian Boyhood</em>, a 1902 memoir by Ohiyesa (Charles A. Eastman), a Santee Dakota writer. In the text, Ohiyesa recalls how the women in his tribe harvested maple syrup during his childhood.</p>
<p style='padding-left: 1em;'>Now the women began to test the trees&mdash;moving leisurely among them, axe in hand, and striking a single quick blow, to see if the sap would appear. <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>The trees, like people, have their individual characters; some were ready to yield up their life-blood, while others were more reluctant.</u></span> Now one of the birchen basins was set under each tree, and a hardwood chip driven deep into the cut which the axe had made. From the corners of this chip&mdash;at first drop by drop, then more freely&mdash;the sap trickled into the little dishes.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ef76c1cc-54c4-4bd6-ad9c-9b120043e21f","content":"<p>It portrays the range of personality traits displayed by the women as they work.</p>"},{"id":"884eddb0-37bd-40e6-8942-26017f823bc9","content":"<p>It foregrounds the beneficial relationship between humans and maple trees.</p>"},{"id":"7c7e1e45-f7a1-456b-92e6-189a1578d619","content":"<p>It demonstrates how human behavior can be influenced by the natural environment.</p>"},{"id":"9c10ddcd-612f-4a86-8e16-e63b817907fa","content":"<p>It elaborates on an aspect of the maple trees that the women evaluate.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9c10ddcd-612f-4a86-8e16-e63b817907fa"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text&rsquo;s overall portrayal of how the women in Ohiyesa&rsquo;s tribe harvested maple syrup. The text states that the women used an axe to strike the maple trees in order to find out which ones would produce sap. The underlined sentence compares the trees to people, with the sap described as the trees&rsquo; &ldquo;life-blood.&rdquo; Some of the trees are ready to give out their sap, while others are unwilling to do so. Using personification, the sentence provides greater detail about the aspect of the maple trees&mdash;their potential to give sap&mdash;that the women are evaluating. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the personalities of the women are not discussed in the text. Although the underlined sentence does mention &ldquo;individual characters,&rdquo; this reference is not to the women in the text but rather to the maple trees, which the sentence compares to people with individual character traits. Choice B is incorrect because the underlined sentence focuses on the trees&rsquo; willingness or refusal to yield sap, not on the beneficial relationship between the women and the trees. Additionally, although the text does suggest that the women and their tribe benefit from the maple trees since the trees allow the women to harvest syrup, there is nothing in the text to suggest that the trees benefit from this relationship in turn. Choice C is incorrect because the underlined sentence is comparing maple trees to humans, not addressing the influence of the natural environment on how the actual humans in the text, the women, behave. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-016-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$41d24e6e-577a-4002-ac33-5fe25e880da2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"570970cd","external_id":"bcba58ca-6a99-4a94-b120-d3a1909d3ad5","vaultid":"4d2acb3a-046f-43a0-a238-0d8adf9cffe8","uId":"954b1912-8588-4645-b40d-01f11f433cee","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from <em>Indian Boyhood</em>, a 1902 memoir by Ohiyesa (Charles A. Eastman), a Santee Dakota writer. In the text, Ohiyesa recalls how the women in his tribe harvested maple syrup during his childhood.</p>\n<p style='padding-left: 1em;'>Now the women began to test the trees&mdash;moving leisurely among them, axe in hand, and striking a single quick blow, to see if the sap would appear. <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>The trees, like people, have their individual characters; some were ready to yield up their life-blood, while others were more reluctant.</u></span> Now one of the birchen basins was set under each tree, and a hardwood chip driven deep into the cut which the axe had made. From the corners of this chip&mdash;at first drop by drop, then more freely&mdash;the sap trickled into the little dishes.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ef76c1cc-54c4-4bd6-ad9c-9b120043e21f","content":"<p>It portrays the range of personality traits displayed by the women as they work.</p>"},{"id":"884eddb0-37bd-40e6-8942-26017f823bc9","content":"<p>It foregrounds the beneficial relationship between humans and maple trees.</p>"},{"id":"7c7e1e45-f7a1-456b-92e6-189a1578d619","content":"<p>It demonstrates how human behavior can be influenced by the natural environment.</p>"},{"id":"9c10ddcd-612f-4a86-8e16-e63b817907fa","content":"<p>It elaborates on an aspect of the maple trees that the women evaluate.</p>"}],"keys":["9c10ddcd-612f-4a86-8e16-e63b817907fa"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text&rsquo;s overall portrayal of how the women in Ohiyesa&rsquo;s tribe harvested maple syrup. The text states that the women used an axe to strike the maple trees in order to find out which ones would produce sap. The underlined sentence compares the trees to people, with the sap described as the trees&rsquo; &ldquo;life-blood.&rdquo; Some of the trees are ready to give out their sap, while others are unwilling to do so. Using personification, the sentence provides greater detail about the aspect of the maple trees&mdash;their potential to give sap&mdash;that the women are evaluating. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the personalities of the women are not discussed in the text. Although the underlined sentence does mention &ldquo;individual characters,&rdquo; this reference is not to the women in the text but rather to the maple trees, which the sentence compares to people with individual character traits. Choice B is incorrect because the underlined sentence focuses on the trees&rsquo; willingness or refusal to yield sap, not on the beneficial relationship between the women and the trees. Additionally, although the text does suggest that the women and their tribe benefit from the maple trees since the trees allow the women to harvest syrup, there is nothing in the text to suggest that the trees benefit from this relationship in turn. Choice C is incorrect because the underlined sentence is comparing maple trees to humans, not addressing the influence of the natural environment on how the actual humans in the text, the women, behave. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-016-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"41d24e6e-577a-4002-ac33-5fe25e880da2","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959316,"updateDate":1691007959316}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959316, 1691007959316),
    ($SATQ$571cf537$SATQ$, $SATQ$300a8942-08ba-43a1-958b-72b8bb8c0a4b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$9f9cac3d-6c51-4cae-a6e0-39bc878ca7d5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The author&rsquo;s claim about the relationship between Neanderthals and <em>Homo sapiens</em> is <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>, as it fails to account for several recent archaeological discoveries. To be convincing, his argument would need to address recent finds of additional hominid fossils, such as the latest Denisovan specimens and <em>Homo longi</em>.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e53ab9e3-54bf-4c50-a560-52fa99f08155","content":"<p>disorienting</p>"},{"id":"09c5d16c-527f-4130-b774-3896b032eb3c","content":"<p>tenuous</p>"},{"id":"070531bc-e46e-436a-abef-d98d6146b825","content":"<p>nuanced</p>"},{"id":"c78d7cbf-8643-4b6d-837b-18b984b1b572","content":"<p>unoriginal</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["09c5d16c-527f-4130-b774-3896b032eb3c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the author&rsquo;s claim about the relationship between Neanderthals and <em>Homo sapiens</em>. As used in this context, &ldquo;tenuous&rdquo; means lacking substance. The end of the first sentence states that the author&rsquo;s claim didn&rsquo;t consider certain key pieces of evidence&mdash;&ldquo;recent archaeological discoveries&rdquo;&mdash;and is therefore weak. &nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context to refer to the author&rsquo;s claim as &ldquo;disorienting,&rdquo; or confusing. The text suggests that the author&rsquo;s claim is insubstantial, not that it&rsquo;s difficult to grasp. Choice C is incorrect because referring to the claim as &ldquo;nuanced,&rdquo; or subtle, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. According to the text, the claim is incomplete because it didn&rsquo;t consider certain key information about recent archaeological finds; it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that what&rsquo;s in the claim lacks precision. Choice D is incorrect because saying that the claim is &ldquo;unoriginal,&rdquo; or imitative, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text faults the claim because it doesn&rsquo;t consider certain key information about recent archaeological finds; it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the author&rsquo;s claim lacks originality. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-972-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$2d663849-fcc8-4e62-a814-ab56fc3af143$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"571cf537","external_id":"300a8942-08ba-43a1-958b-72b8bb8c0a4b","vaultid":"9f9cac3d-6c51-4cae-a6e0-39bc878ca7d5","uId":"f0aeea6d-c157-4fc1-9643-9959ea0b3ce2","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The author&rsquo;s claim about the relationship between Neanderthals and <em>Homo sapiens</em> is <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>, as it fails to account for several recent archaeological discoveries. To be convincing, his argument would need to address recent finds of additional hominid fossils, such as the latest Denisovan specimens and <em>Homo longi</em>.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e53ab9e3-54bf-4c50-a560-52fa99f08155","content":"<p>disorienting</p>"},{"id":"09c5d16c-527f-4130-b774-3896b032eb3c","content":"<p>tenuous</p>"},{"id":"070531bc-e46e-436a-abef-d98d6146b825","content":"<p>nuanced</p>"},{"id":"c78d7cbf-8643-4b6d-837b-18b984b1b572","content":"<p>unoriginal</p>"}],"keys":["09c5d16c-527f-4130-b774-3896b032eb3c"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the author&rsquo;s claim about the relationship between Neanderthals and <em>Homo sapiens</em>. As used in this context, &ldquo;tenuous&rdquo; means lacking substance. The end of the first sentence states that the author&rsquo;s claim didn&rsquo;t consider certain key pieces of evidence&mdash;&ldquo;recent archaeological discoveries&rdquo;&mdash;and is therefore weak. &nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context to refer to the author&rsquo;s claim as &ldquo;disorienting,&rdquo; or confusing. The text suggests that the author&rsquo;s claim is insubstantial, not that it&rsquo;s difficult to grasp. Choice C is incorrect because referring to the claim as &ldquo;nuanced,&rdquo; or subtle, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. According to the text, the claim is incomplete because it didn&rsquo;t consider certain key information about recent archaeological finds; it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that what&rsquo;s in the claim lacks precision. Choice D is incorrect because saying that the claim is &ldquo;unoriginal,&rdquo; or imitative, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text faults the claim because it doesn&rsquo;t consider certain key information about recent archaeological finds; it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the author&rsquo;s claim lacks originality. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-972-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"2d663849-fcc8-4e62-a814-ab56fc3af143","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959357,"updateDate":1691007959357}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959357, 1691007959357),
    ($SATQ$590f0ad2$SATQ$, $SATQ$5ad6a8d1-72f4-46c4-a14a-7776f9c916f6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$c829c6df-fe0d-4a67-82e7-04f88278bd1f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Industrial activity is often assumed to be a threat to wildlife, but that isn&rsquo;t always so. <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>Consider the silver-studded blue butterfly (<em>Plebejus argus</em>)</u></span>: as forest growth has reduced grasslands in northern Germany, many of these butterflies have left meadow habitats and are now thriving in active limestone quarries. In a survey of multiple active quarries and patches of maintained grassland, an ecologist found silver-studded blue butterflies in 100% of the quarries but only 57% of the grassland patches. Moreover, butterfly populations in the quarries were four times larger than those in the meadows.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"65a3ce66-d63d-4b13-81c3-ac88bcd8a022","content":"<p>It challenges a common assumption about the species under investigation in the research referred to in the text.</p>"},{"id":"c9979039-61a5-4051-bcec-5cd526ea0e91","content":"<p>It introduces discussion of a specific example that supports the general claim made in the previous sentence.</p>"},{"id":"b15e0c75-fba4-4990-a955-6983f773dc4d","content":"<p>It suggests that a certain species should be included in additional studies like the one mentioned later in the text.</p>"},{"id":"a6c2e055-2778-447d-8e7e-b79d8ebf6e40","content":"<p>It provides a definition for an unfamiliar term that is central to the main argument in the text.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c9979039-61a5-4051-bcec-5cd526ea0e91"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most accurately describes how the underlined portion functions in the text as a whole. The first sentence presents the general claim that industrial activity is not always a threat to wildlife. The underlined portion of the sentence that follows suggests that the silver-studded blue butterfly is an example of wildlife thriving in areas of industrial activity: active limestone quarries. Thus, the function of the underlined portion is to introduce a specific example in support of the general claim in the previous sentence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the first sentence indicates that &ldquo;industrial activity is often assumed&rdquo; to harm wildlife, in the case of the silver-studded blue butterfly the text mentions neither an assumption about this species nor any challenge to such an assumption. Choice C is incorrect because the text mentions only one study: the &ldquo;survey.&rdquo; Additional studies are not mentioned in the text. Choice D is incorrect because neither the underlined portion nor any other portion of the text provides a definition for any of the terms used in the text&rsquo;s argument. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-006-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c547c69d-b911-42be-82b8-7e333129ffe6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"590f0ad2","external_id":"5ad6a8d1-72f4-46c4-a14a-7776f9c916f6","vaultid":"c829c6df-fe0d-4a67-82e7-04f88278bd1f","uId":"cde63301-7c85-42df-894a-eef1fe02b78e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Industrial activity is often assumed to be a threat to wildlife, but that isn&rsquo;t always so. <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>Consider the silver-studded blue butterfly (<em>Plebejus argus</em>)</u></span>: as forest growth has reduced grasslands in northern Germany, many of these butterflies have left meadow habitats and are now thriving in active limestone quarries. In a survey of multiple active quarries and patches of maintained grassland, an ecologist found silver-studded blue butterflies in 100% of the quarries but only 57% of the grassland patches. Moreover, butterfly populations in the quarries were four times larger than those in the meadows.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"65a3ce66-d63d-4b13-81c3-ac88bcd8a022","content":"<p>It challenges a common assumption about the species under investigation in the research referred to in the text.</p>"},{"id":"c9979039-61a5-4051-bcec-5cd526ea0e91","content":"<p>It introduces discussion of a specific example that supports the general claim made in the previous sentence.</p>"},{"id":"b15e0c75-fba4-4990-a955-6983f773dc4d","content":"<p>It suggests that a certain species should be included in additional studies like the one mentioned later in the text.</p>"},{"id":"a6c2e055-2778-447d-8e7e-b79d8ebf6e40","content":"<p>It provides a definition for an unfamiliar term that is central to the main argument in the text.</p>"}],"keys":["c9979039-61a5-4051-bcec-5cd526ea0e91"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most accurately describes how the underlined portion functions in the text as a whole. The first sentence presents the general claim that industrial activity is not always a threat to wildlife. The underlined portion of the sentence that follows suggests that the silver-studded blue butterfly is an example of wildlife thriving in areas of industrial activity: active limestone quarries. Thus, the function of the underlined portion is to introduce a specific example in support of the general claim in the previous sentence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the first sentence indicates that &ldquo;industrial activity is often assumed&rdquo; to harm wildlife, in the case of the silver-studded blue butterfly the text mentions neither an assumption about this species nor any challenge to such an assumption. Choice C is incorrect because the text mentions only one study: the &ldquo;survey.&rdquo; Additional studies are not mentioned in the text. Choice D is incorrect because neither the underlined portion nor any other portion of the text provides a definition for any of the terms used in the text&rsquo;s argument. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-006-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c547c69d-b911-42be-82b8-7e333129ffe6","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959313,"updateDate":1691007959313}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959313, 1691007959313),
    ($SATQ$592f94c2$SATQ$, $SATQ$51519b20-3a00-4154-8ba3-8d08a2b8fc66$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><span class="paired_passage" role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 1&nbsp;</strong></span></p>
<p>Good art often challenges and disrupts social and aesthetic norms, but the creation of public art&mdash;paintings, sculptures, and performance pieces displayed in nonmuseum or nontheatrical public settings&mdash;typically requires broad agreement among artists, civic officials, and community members about the works&rsquo; message and artistic goals. <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">Public art that fails to appease everyone by being sufficiently aesthetically and conceptually bland almost inevitably provokes backlash.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span class="paired_passage" role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>Public art is commonly displayed in spaces intended for purposes other than meaningful aesthetic engagement. Some critics of public art therefore note that norm-defying pieces that aren&rsquo;t effectively integrated within their surroundings in a manner that primes passersby to appreciate the pieces&rsquo; merits (as is often the case) tend to be regarded more unfavorably than similarly provocative art encountered in museums is.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would the critics mentioned in Text 2 most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"20f3df5f-0c11-4b74-b592-81828ed08f81","content":"<p>By arguing that the reason members of the general public might disagree about a public artwork&rsquo;s merits is unrelated to the unconventionality of its appearance and ideas&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"51ecbf69-0b75-4106-9d07-0fb91f195ca1","content":"<p>By agreeing with the idea that only works of art that are universally appealing are suitable for displaying in public spaces</p>"},{"id":"3c1a6029-1cb9-49c8-9c77-ae91d0b4aacf","content":"<p>By disputing the notion that civic leaders and community members are easily placated by art that is intended mainly to reinforce social norms</p>"},{"id":"ac135779-f026-490b-aabf-df48c4b4121f","content":"<p>By contending that the kinds of reactions controversial public artworks often receive aren&rsquo;t exclusively the result of attributes inherent in the works themselves&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ac135779-f026-490b-aabf-df48c4b4121f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because the critics mentioned in Text 2 would argue that negative reactions to controversial public art are not solely the result of the nature of the art itself. The claim in Text 1 suggests that public artworks tend to provoke backlash when they are not &ldquo;aesthetically and conceptually bland.&rdquo; This implies that the content of public art alone can cause backlash. However, the critics in Text 2 offer a more nuanced explanation. They argue that norm-defying pieces of public art that are not &ldquo;effectively integrated within their surroundings&rdquo; are more likely to be judged harshly by the public. They also imply that a museum provides a context that prepares viewers to expect challenging art, while public environments that serve other purposes often do not. This indicates that the critics in Text 2 would contend that factors beyond the attributes of the art, like environment and presentation, play a significant role in how public art is received.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the critics in Text 2 do not argue that unconventionality is unrelated to public disagreement about a public artwork&rsquo;s merits. Rather, they acknowledge that norm-defying pieces do face unfavorable reactions, but they attribute such reactions to contextual factors like poor integration with the environment rather than dismissing the role of unconventionality entirely. Choice B is incorrect because the critics in Text 2 do not advocate for restricting public art to universally appealing works. Instead, they argue that better integration with the surroundings and situating the work in appropriate contexts could help norm-defying art be better received by passersby. Choice C is incorrect because the critics in Text 2 do not address whether civic leaders and community members are easily satisfied by norm-reinforcing art. The critics in Text 2 instead argue that installation and integration with the surrounding environment factor into how norm-defying art is received by the general public, rather than disputing how people respond to conventional art.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"592f94c2","external_id":"51519b20-3a00-4154-8ba3-8d08a2b8fc66","vaultid":null,"uId":"78dbceed-ebea-4c59-8e6d-d654d6cd6742","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><span class=\"paired_passage\" role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 1&nbsp;</strong></span></p>\n<p>Good art often challenges and disrupts social and aesthetic norms, but the creation of public art&mdash;paintings, sculptures, and performance pieces displayed in nonmuseum or nontheatrical public settings&mdash;typically requires broad agreement among artists, civic officials, and community members about the works&rsquo; message and artistic goals. <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">Public art that fails to appease everyone by being sufficiently aesthetically and conceptually bland almost inevitably provokes backlash.</span></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><span class=\"paired_passage\" role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>Public art is commonly displayed in spaces intended for purposes other than meaningful aesthetic engagement. Some critics of public art therefore note that norm-defying pieces that aren&rsquo;t effectively integrated within their surroundings in a manner that primes passersby to appreciate the pieces&rsquo; merits (as is often the case) tend to be regarded more unfavorably than similarly provocative art encountered in museums is.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would the critics mentioned in Text 2 most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"20f3df5f-0c11-4b74-b592-81828ed08f81","content":"<p>By arguing that the reason members of the general public might disagree about a public artwork&rsquo;s merits is unrelated to the unconventionality of its appearance and ideas&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"51ecbf69-0b75-4106-9d07-0fb91f195ca1","content":"<p>By agreeing with the idea that only works of art that are universally appealing are suitable for displaying in public spaces</p>"},{"id":"3c1a6029-1cb9-49c8-9c77-ae91d0b4aacf","content":"<p>By disputing the notion that civic leaders and community members are easily placated by art that is intended mainly to reinforce social norms</p>"},{"id":"ac135779-f026-490b-aabf-df48c4b4121f","content":"<p>By contending that the kinds of reactions controversial public artworks often receive aren&rsquo;t exclusively the result of attributes inherent in the works themselves&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["ac135779-f026-490b-aabf-df48c4b4121f"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because the critics mentioned in Text 2 would argue that negative reactions to controversial public art are not solely the result of the nature of the art itself. The claim in Text 1 suggests that public artworks tend to provoke backlash when they are not &ldquo;aesthetically and conceptually bland.&rdquo; This implies that the content of public art alone can cause backlash. However, the critics in Text 2 offer a more nuanced explanation. They argue that norm-defying pieces of public art that are not &ldquo;effectively integrated within their surroundings&rdquo; are more likely to be judged harshly by the public. They also imply that a museum provides a context that prepares viewers to expect challenging art, while public environments that serve other purposes often do not. This indicates that the critics in Text 2 would contend that factors beyond the attributes of the art, like environment and presentation, play a significant role in how public art is received.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the critics in Text 2 do not argue that unconventionality is unrelated to public disagreement about a public artwork&rsquo;s merits. Rather, they acknowledge that norm-defying pieces do face unfavorable reactions, but they attribute such reactions to contextual factors like poor integration with the environment rather than dismissing the role of unconventionality entirely. Choice B is incorrect because the critics in Text 2 do not advocate for restricting public art to universally appealing works. Instead, they argue that better integration with the surroundings and situating the work in appropriate contexts could help norm-defying art be better received by passersby. Choice C is incorrect because the critics in Text 2 do not address whether civic leaders and community members are easily satisfied by norm-reinforcing art. The critics in Text 2 instead argue that installation and integration with the surrounding environment factor into how norm-defying art is received by the general public, rather than disputing how people respond to conventional art.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771168,"updateDate":1776362771168}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771168, 1776362771168),
    ($SATQ$5a278f24$SATQ$, $SATQ$0295cfad-2b39-4295-b361-841f00e1c7a6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$a296c43b-f7d5-4285-92fe-daf648bfa179$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The work of molecular biophysicist Enrique M. De La Cruz is known for <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> traditional boundaries between academic disciplines. The university laboratory that De La Cruz runs includes engineers, biologists, chemists, and physicists, and the research the lab produces makes use of insights and techniques from all those fields.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b5d36e39-1a21-4f99-a0fa-f72268f80caa","content":"<p>epitomizing</p>"},{"id":"bfec0fa7-5814-4d8e-8a0a-3b569457e8c4","content":"<p>transcending</p>"},{"id":"bac47d4e-f4af-4aae-839a-d12ce08635c4","content":"<p>anticipating</p>"},{"id":"cf0c07b6-179e-49ee-905c-02ec649a79ff","content":"<p>reinforcing</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["bfec0fa7-5814-4d8e-8a0a-3b569457e8c4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. Based on the text, we&rsquo;re looking for a word that means something similar to "mak[ing] use of insights and techniques from all those fields." "Transcending" means "going beyond," so "transcending traditional boundaries" would mean crossing into all those various fields of research, which is exactly the meaning we want.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t a logical word choice. Based on the text, we&rsquo;re looking for a word that means something similar to "mak[ing] use of insights and techniques from all those fields." To "epitomize" means to "be a perfect example of," so "epitomizing traditional boundaries" would mean the opposite of what we want: keeping the fields of research separate. Choice C is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t a logical word choice. Based on the text, we&rsquo;re looking for a word that means something similar to "mak[ing] use of insights and techniques from all those fields." "Anticipating" means "expecting" or "waiting for," and would result in a confusing sentence with an unclear meaning. Choice D is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t a logical word choice. Based on the text, we&rsquo;re looking for a word that means something similar to "mak[ing] use of insights and techniques from all those fields." "Reinforcing traditional boundaries" would mean the opposite: keeping the fields of research separate.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-910-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$165c05bf-20cb-4196-a1c1-4df9a0891d09$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5a278f24","external_id":"0295cfad-2b39-4295-b361-841f00e1c7a6","vaultid":"a296c43b-f7d5-4285-92fe-daf648bfa179","uId":"3310e10e-da8c-4520-88cb-982b05236c7f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The work of molecular biophysicist Enrique M. De La Cruz is known for <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> traditional boundaries between academic disciplines. The university laboratory that De La Cruz runs includes engineers, biologists, chemists, and physicists, and the research the lab produces makes use of insights and techniques from all those fields.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b5d36e39-1a21-4f99-a0fa-f72268f80caa","content":"<p>epitomizing</p>"},{"id":"bfec0fa7-5814-4d8e-8a0a-3b569457e8c4","content":"<p>transcending</p>"},{"id":"bac47d4e-f4af-4aae-839a-d12ce08635c4","content":"<p>anticipating</p>"},{"id":"cf0c07b6-179e-49ee-905c-02ec649a79ff","content":"<p>reinforcing</p>"}],"keys":["bfec0fa7-5814-4d8e-8a0a-3b569457e8c4"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. Based on the text, we&rsquo;re looking for a word that means something similar to \"mak[ing] use of insights and techniques from all those fields.\" \"Transcending\" means \"going beyond,\" so \"transcending traditional boundaries\" would mean crossing into all those various fields of research, which is exactly the meaning we want.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t a logical word choice. Based on the text, we&rsquo;re looking for a word that means something similar to \"mak[ing] use of insights and techniques from all those fields.\" To \"epitomize\" means to \"be a perfect example of,\" so \"epitomizing traditional boundaries\" would mean the opposite of what we want: keeping the fields of research separate. Choice C is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t a logical word choice. Based on the text, we&rsquo;re looking for a word that means something similar to \"mak[ing] use of insights and techniques from all those fields.\" \"Anticipating\" means \"expecting\" or \"waiting for,\" and would result in a confusing sentence with an unclear meaning. Choice D is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t a logical word choice. Based on the text, we&rsquo;re looking for a word that means something similar to \"mak[ing] use of insights and techniques from all those fields.\" \"Reinforcing traditional boundaries\" would mean the opposite: keeping the fields of research separate.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-910-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"165c05bf-20cb-4196-a1c1-4df9a0891d09","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959354,"updateDate":1691007959354}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959354, 1691007959354),
    ($SATQ$5a4b147c$SATQ$, $SATQ$32674912-212e-4fe3-bbee-05839397bbaf$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e6281159-b86c-4524-9ee2-f3e1c082071b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><strong>Text 1</strong></p>
<p>On April 26th, 1777, Sybil Ludington rode 40 miles by horse through Putnam County, New York, to gather up local militia. British forces were burning nearby Danbury, Connecticut, and Ludington wanted to rally rebel troops to meet them. Although she was only 16 years old at the time, her brave feat made Ludington one of the heroes of the American Revolution. Since then, Ludington has been widely celebrated, inspiring postage stamps, statues, and even children&rsquo;s TV series.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Text 2</strong></p>
<p>Historian Paula D. Hunt researched the life and legacy of Sybil Ludington but found no evidence for her famous ride. Although many articles and books have been written about Ludington, Hunt believes writers may have been inventing details about Ludington as they retold her story. Ludington is revered by Americans today, but there simply isn&rsquo;t a strong historical record of her heroic ride.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, both authors would most likely agree with which statement?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b0e365fc-a1ca-4c19-ac58-17c62ca67ebd","content":"<p>Sybil Ludington was crucial to the outcome of the Revolutionary War.</p>"},{"id":"9975e909-1866-4f26-820b-1066a97b4245","content":"<p>Historians have confirmed which route Sybil Ludington took.</p>"},{"id":"e0c4560b-01bf-4525-ae47-f55ba9de9128","content":"<p>Sybil Ludington was likely not a real person.</p>"},{"id":"eb5f8151-4547-4d6e-bbeb-0c118b4c0910","content":"<p>Many people have come to admire the story of Sybil Ludington&rsquo;s ride.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["eb5f8151-4547-4d6e-bbeb-0c118b4c0910"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. Both authors agree that Ludington&rsquo;s story has been widely celebrated and revered by Americans, even if they disagree on its accuracy. Text 1 states that Ludington has inspired postage stamps, statues, and TV series, and Text 2 states that many articles and books have been written about her. Thus, both authors acknowledge the popularity of Ludington&rsquo;s story. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Neither author claims that Ludington had a significant impact on the war. Text 1 doesn&rsquo;t mention how Ludington&rsquo;s ride affected the war overall, and Text 2 suggests that Ludington&rsquo;s ride may have been exaggerated or invented over time. Choice B is incorrect. Neither author claims that Ludington&rsquo;s route has been verified by historians. Text 1 claims she rode 40 miles through Putnam County, but doesn&rsquo;t cite any sources for this information, while Text 2 suggests the ride may not have even happened. Choice C is incorrect. This choice misreads text 2. Neither author claims that Ludington was not a real person, only that her ride may not have happened. Both authors seem to treat Ludington as a genuine historical figure. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CTC-E008$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$bd34986e-063b-4926-978f-e06d22cd70a8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5a4b147c","external_id":"32674912-212e-4fe3-bbee-05839397bbaf","vaultid":"e6281159-b86c-4524-9ee2-f3e1c082071b","uId":"7437cb26-e342-484b-8c80-0a0c5dc9201c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><strong>Text 1</strong></p>\n<p>On April 26th, 1777, Sybil Ludington rode 40 miles by horse through Putnam County, New York, to gather up local militia. British forces were burning nearby Danbury, Connecticut, and Ludington wanted to rally rebel troops to meet them. Although she was only 16 years old at the time, her brave feat made Ludington one of the heroes of the American Revolution. Since then, Ludington has been widely celebrated, inspiring postage stamps, statues, and even children&rsquo;s TV series.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Text 2</strong></p>\n<p>Historian Paula D. Hunt researched the life and legacy of Sybil Ludington but found no evidence for her famous ride. Although many articles and books have been written about Ludington, Hunt believes writers may have been inventing details about Ludington as they retold her story. Ludington is revered by Americans today, but there simply isn&rsquo;t a strong historical record of her heroic ride.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, both authors would most likely agree with which statement?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b0e365fc-a1ca-4c19-ac58-17c62ca67ebd","content":"<p>Sybil Ludington was crucial to the outcome of the Revolutionary War.</p>"},{"id":"9975e909-1866-4f26-820b-1066a97b4245","content":"<p>Historians have confirmed which route Sybil Ludington took.</p>"},{"id":"e0c4560b-01bf-4525-ae47-f55ba9de9128","content":"<p>Sybil Ludington was likely not a real person.</p>"},{"id":"eb5f8151-4547-4d6e-bbeb-0c118b4c0910","content":"<p>Many people have come to admire the story of Sybil Ludington&rsquo;s ride.</p>"}],"keys":["eb5f8151-4547-4d6e-bbeb-0c118b4c0910"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. Both authors agree that Ludington&rsquo;s story has been widely celebrated and revered by Americans, even if they disagree on its accuracy. Text 1 states that Ludington has inspired postage stamps, statues, and TV series, and Text 2 states that many articles and books have been written about her. Thus, both authors acknowledge the popularity of Ludington&rsquo;s story. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Neither author claims that Ludington had a significant impact on the war. Text 1 doesn&rsquo;t mention how Ludington&rsquo;s ride affected the war overall, and Text 2 suggests that Ludington&rsquo;s ride may have been exaggerated or invented over time. Choice B is incorrect. Neither author claims that Ludington&rsquo;s route has been verified by historians. Text 1 claims she rode 40 miles through Putnam County, but doesn&rsquo;t cite any sources for this information, while Text 2 suggests the ride may not have even happened. Choice C is incorrect. This choice misreads text 2. Neither author claims that Ludington was not a real person, only that her ride may not have happened. Both authors seem to treat Ludington as a genuine historical figure. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CTC-E008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"bd34986e-063b-4926-978f-e06d22cd70a8","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959361,"updateDate":1691007959361}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959361, 1691007959361),
    ($SATQ$5a97d9cd$SATQ$, $SATQ$0443f76e-d72d-41ed-b1a7-212994193aad$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In a study of residential mobility in Washington, DC, LaTanya Brown-Robertson et al. found that increases in household spending power (via tax credits) had mixed effects on mobility decisions, possibly because residential location choices are not fully reducible to economic factors but are also <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> noneconomic factors such as access to strong local social networks.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4ffc6d9a-20a0-47ec-af07-281e2dadc6dc","content":"<p>transposed with</p>"},{"id":"462c99c4-65fc-4f1e-aeb0-d2e849c68300","content":"<p>mediated by</p>"},{"id":"4336300e-36c2-4ed1-95dc-60ed8f40cd6c","content":"<p>decoupled from</p>"},{"id":"e67a12cd-347f-40d5-bebb-08b616c510c4","content":"<p>antecedent to</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["462c99c4-65fc-4f1e-aeb0-d2e849c68300"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of residential mobility decisions. In this context, "mediated by" means influenced or affected by something. The text establishes that increasing household spending power (an economic factor) produced "mixed effects" on mobility decisions. The text then explains that residential choices are "not fully reducible to economic factors," which means that these decisions don&rsquo;t simply rely on how much money is available to spend on a residential move but on other factors as well. According to the text, noneconomic considerations, such as access to social networks, also play a significant role. Thus, "mediated by" accurately captures this relationship where noneconomic factors influence what might otherwise be purely economic decisions.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "transposed with" would suggest that economic and noneconomic factors are being switched or interchanged when making residential mobility decisions, which wouldn&rsquo;t make logical sense in this context. The text indicates that these factors coexist and jointly influence such decisions. Choice C is incorrect. The phrase "decoupled from" means separated or disconnected from, which would suggest that economic factors are completely separate from noneconomic factors. This would contradict the text&rsquo;s main point that residential mobility decisions involve multiple factors being considered simultaneously rather than in isolation. Choice D is incorrect because "antecedent to," which means something prior or earlier, would indicate that noneconomic factors come before economic ones in time or sequence. The text doesn&rsquo;t establish this chronological relationship. Instead, the text indicates that economic factors (household spending power) and noneconomic factors (access to social networks) simultaneously influence residential mobility decisions.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5a97d9cd","external_id":"0443f76e-d72d-41ed-b1a7-212994193aad","vaultid":null,"uId":"239db9d1-3876-4057-92df-abc7b69482ab","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In a study of residential mobility in Washington, DC, LaTanya Brown-Robertson et al. found that increases in household spending power (via tax credits) had mixed effects on mobility decisions, possibly because residential location choices are not fully reducible to economic factors but are also <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> noneconomic factors such as access to strong local social networks.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4ffc6d9a-20a0-47ec-af07-281e2dadc6dc","content":"<p>transposed with</p>"},{"id":"462c99c4-65fc-4f1e-aeb0-d2e849c68300","content":"<p>mediated by</p>"},{"id":"4336300e-36c2-4ed1-95dc-60ed8f40cd6c","content":"<p>decoupled from</p>"},{"id":"e67a12cd-347f-40d5-bebb-08b616c510c4","content":"<p>antecedent to</p>"}],"keys":["462c99c4-65fc-4f1e-aeb0-d2e849c68300"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of residential mobility decisions. In this context, \"mediated by\" means influenced or affected by something. The text establishes that increasing household spending power (an economic factor) produced \"mixed effects\" on mobility decisions. The text then explains that residential choices are \"not fully reducible to economic factors,\" which means that these decisions don&rsquo;t simply rely on how much money is available to spend on a residential move but on other factors as well. According to the text, noneconomic considerations, such as access to social networks, also play a significant role. Thus, \"mediated by\" accurately captures this relationship where noneconomic factors influence what might otherwise be purely economic decisions.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"transposed with\" would suggest that economic and noneconomic factors are being switched or interchanged when making residential mobility decisions, which wouldn&rsquo;t make logical sense in this context. The text indicates that these factors coexist and jointly influence such decisions. Choice C is incorrect. The phrase \"decoupled from\" means separated or disconnected from, which would suggest that economic factors are completely separate from noneconomic factors. This would contradict the text&rsquo;s main point that residential mobility decisions involve multiple factors being considered simultaneously rather than in isolation. Choice D is incorrect because \"antecedent to,\" which means something prior or earlier, would indicate that noneconomic factors come before economic ones in time or sequence. The text doesn&rsquo;t establish this chronological relationship. Instead, the text indicates that economic factors (household spending power) and noneconomic factors (access to social networks) simultaneously influence residential mobility decisions.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061175,"updateDate":1755117061175}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061175, 1755117061175),
    ($SATQ$5d2fd27d$SATQ$, $SATQ$76d6d4be-31c1-4303-8399-9cab44b67135$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f9e006d4-97dd-4699-b315-46fa4533b693$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While we can infer information about climate activity in Earth&rsquo;s distant past from physical evidence, we of course cannot observe past climates directly. To study early Earth&rsquo;s climate in action, we must <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> that climate using computer models that represent various climate conditions consistent with the physical evidence.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d26e69bc-0ea0-4f00-ad0c-c3f3baee5662","content":"<p>invent</p>"},{"id":"1d07a6f2-5619-468a-bd06-478fda3158a2","content":"<p>simulate</p>"},{"id":"055df5d7-a680-4669-97f5-b8e1684cfda6","content":"<p>exaggerate</p>"},{"id":"89ba798f-f8b6-471a-9977-2961b319012d","content":"<p>preserve</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1d07a6f2-5619-468a-bd06-478fda3158a2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of climate activity in Earth&rsquo;s distant past. In this context, to &ldquo;simulate&rdquo; most nearly means to represent a natural process using computer models. According to the text, understanding Earth&rsquo;s early climate is difficult because we cannot make direct observations of the distant past. Instead, scientists must create computer models that approximate early climate conditions, based on the physical evidence that those conditions left behind on Earth&rsquo;s surface. This context supports the idea that computer models simulate Earth&rsquo;s climate in the distant past. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because scientists use existing physical evidence&nbsp;as a basis for developing computer models that describe what Earth&rsquo;s actual past climate might have been like. The models are not being used to &ldquo;invent,&rdquo; or imagine, a completely fictional climate. Choice C is incorrect because the computer models are being used in an attempt to describe as accurately as possible what Earth&rsquo;s past climate might have been like. They are not attempting to &ldquo;exaggerate,&rdquo; or distort, those features. Choice D is incorrect because the computer models do not &ldquo;preserve,&rdquo; or protect from deterioration, Earth&rsquo;s early climate; instead, they attempt to reproduce the characteristics of that climate, based on the remaining physical evidence of that climate. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-911-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$77af84a3-7611-4dd0-921b-bae43296a53f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5d2fd27d","external_id":"76d6d4be-31c1-4303-8399-9cab44b67135","vaultid":"f9e006d4-97dd-4699-b315-46fa4533b693","uId":"50f54a42-908d-43cf-bcb9-6e395d4cea1e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While we can infer information about climate activity in Earth&rsquo;s distant past from physical evidence, we of course cannot observe past climates directly. To study early Earth&rsquo;s climate in action, we must <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> that climate using computer models that represent various climate conditions consistent with the physical evidence.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d26e69bc-0ea0-4f00-ad0c-c3f3baee5662","content":"<p>invent</p>"},{"id":"1d07a6f2-5619-468a-bd06-478fda3158a2","content":"<p>simulate</p>"},{"id":"055df5d7-a680-4669-97f5-b8e1684cfda6","content":"<p>exaggerate</p>"},{"id":"89ba798f-f8b6-471a-9977-2961b319012d","content":"<p>preserve</p>"}],"keys":["1d07a6f2-5619-468a-bd06-478fda3158a2"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of climate activity in Earth&rsquo;s distant past. In this context, to &ldquo;simulate&rdquo; most nearly means to represent a natural process using computer models. According to the text, understanding Earth&rsquo;s early climate is difficult because we cannot make direct observations of the distant past. Instead, scientists must create computer models that approximate early climate conditions, based on the physical evidence that those conditions left behind on Earth&rsquo;s surface. This context supports the idea that computer models simulate Earth&rsquo;s climate in the distant past. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because scientists use existing physical evidence&nbsp;as a basis for developing computer models that describe what Earth&rsquo;s actual past climate might have been like. The models are not being used to &ldquo;invent,&rdquo; or imagine, a completely fictional climate. Choice C is incorrect because the computer models are being used in an attempt to describe as accurately as possible what Earth&rsquo;s past climate might have been like. They are not attempting to &ldquo;exaggerate,&rdquo; or distort, those features. Choice D is incorrect because the computer models do not &ldquo;preserve,&rdquo; or protect from deterioration, Earth&rsquo;s early climate; instead, they attempt to reproduce the characteristics of that climate, based on the remaining physical evidence of that climate. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-911-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"77af84a3-7611-4dd0-921b-bae43296a53f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959354,"updateDate":1691007959354}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959354, 1691007959354),
    ($SATQ$5d48f889$SATQ$, $SATQ$87db806c-1c2d-4f5c-ae29-5a904de96673$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Mercedes de Acosta&rsquo;s 1921 poem &ldquo;Spring and You.&rdquo;</p>
<blockquote class="dap_poemexcerpt">
<p>Now, Love and April, and the gold of your</p>
<p>&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;hair,</p>
<p>Are all <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>mingled</u></span> together</p>
<p>Like the blending of an exotic dream plant</p>
<p>With the fragrant perfume of a strange, frail</p>
<p>&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;flower.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;mingled&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a19fcd42-f4f1-4f8b-8486-1ad034148fa2","content":"<p>Identified</p>"},{"id":"4bbe0475-1f37-4176-96f6-c3c51b439035","content":"<p>Combined</p>"},{"id":"2603b2c0-30f8-4897-b970-3b27c1bbecc8","content":"<p>Remembered</p>"},{"id":"4b615bb1-084c-4496-b427-de2f8f30dead","content":"<p>Celebrated</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4bbe0475-1f37-4176-96f6-c3c51b439035"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because as used in the text, "mingled" most nearly means combined. In the text, the speaker refers to several concepts mingling together and then compares this to "the blending of" two other things (an exotic plant and the fragrance of a strange flower). This comparison conveys that the speaker views the concepts of love, April, and the gold of someone&rsquo;s hair as being blended together, or combined.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "mingled" means combined, not identified. The speaker names several concepts (love, April, and the gold color of someone&rsquo;s hair) and then moves to a comparison of their mingling together to "the blending of" a plant and the scent of a flower, conveying the idea that the speaker views the concepts as blended together, or combined. Choice C is incorrect. The text as a whole may refer to a memory, but the comparison of the mingling of several concepts (love, April, and the gold color of someone&rsquo;s hair) to "the blending of" a plant and the scent of a flower conveys that the speaker views those concepts as actually blended together, or combined, and not simply remembered at the same time. Choice D is incorrect. The speaker refers to several concepts (love, April, and the gold color of someone&rsquo;s hair) and then compares the mingling together of those concepts to "the blending of" a plant and the scent of a flower; rather than suggesting that the concepts are celebrated, this comparison conveys that the speaker views the concepts as blended together, or combined.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5d48f889","external_id":"87db806c-1c2d-4f5c-ae29-5a904de96673","vaultid":null,"uId":"fd219df0-a492-4abb-9e50-647f6cc512d1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Mercedes de Acosta&rsquo;s 1921 poem &ldquo;Spring and You.&rdquo;</p>\n<blockquote class=\"dap_poemexcerpt\">\n<p>Now, Love and April, and the gold of your</p>\n<p>&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;hair,</p>\n<p>Are all <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>mingled</u></span> together</p>\n<p>Like the blending of an exotic dream plant</p>\n<p>With the fragrant perfume of a strange, frail</p>\n<p>&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;&ensp;flower.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;mingled&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a19fcd42-f4f1-4f8b-8486-1ad034148fa2","content":"<p>Identified</p>"},{"id":"4bbe0475-1f37-4176-96f6-c3c51b439035","content":"<p>Combined</p>"},{"id":"2603b2c0-30f8-4897-b970-3b27c1bbecc8","content":"<p>Remembered</p>"},{"id":"4b615bb1-084c-4496-b427-de2f8f30dead","content":"<p>Celebrated</p>"}],"keys":["4bbe0475-1f37-4176-96f6-c3c51b439035"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because as used in the text, \"mingled\" most nearly means combined. In the text, the speaker refers to several concepts mingling together and then compares this to \"the blending of\" two other things (an exotic plant and the fragrance of a strange flower). This comparison conveys that the speaker views the concepts of love, April, and the gold of someone&rsquo;s hair as being blended together, or combined.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"mingled\" means combined, not identified. The speaker names several concepts (love, April, and the gold color of someone&rsquo;s hair) and then moves to a comparison of their mingling together to \"the blending of\" a plant and the scent of a flower, conveying the idea that the speaker views the concepts as blended together, or combined. Choice C is incorrect. The text as a whole may refer to a memory, but the comparison of the mingling of several concepts (love, April, and the gold color of someone&rsquo;s hair) to \"the blending of\" a plant and the scent of a flower conveys that the speaker views those concepts as actually blended together, or combined, and not simply remembered at the same time. Choice D is incorrect. The speaker refers to several concepts (love, April, and the gold color of someone&rsquo;s hair) and then compares the mingling together of those concepts to \"the blending of\" a plant and the scent of a flower; rather than suggesting that the concepts are celebrated, this comparison conveys that the speaker views the concepts as blended together, or combined.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323031,"updateDate":1730147323031}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323031, 1730147323031),
    ($SATQ$5dce6cab$SATQ$, $SATQ$bad731cb-eb93-479c-8a7b-c33e8f78e7b0$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$cb2cde63-3ef8-46d4-8b1a-2e63748e2608$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Given that the conditions in binary star systems should make planetary formation nearly impossible, it&rsquo;s not surprising that the existence of planets in such systems has lacked <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> explanation. Roman Rafikov and Kedron Silsbee shed light on the subject when they used modeling to determine a complex set of factors that could support planets&rsquo; development.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b8c72143-7d3e-4e19-90cc-95395dd1e7c6","content":"<p>a discernible</p>"},{"id":"a7c26dee-0e90-49c8-acf9-9edeb237813b","content":"<p>a straightforward</p>"},{"id":"0c49ec74-e790-4479-858f-726e6e2584bb","content":"<p>an inconclusive</p>"},{"id":"5b8f2beb-5d25-4cd6-bbe2-cbaf69ed7f02","content":"<p>an unbiased</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a7c26dee-0e90-49c8-acf9-9edeb237813b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s description of efforts to explain the existence of planets in binary star systems. As used in this context, describing an explanation as &ldquo;a straightforward&rdquo; one would mean that the explanation is direct and uncomplicated. The text asserts that since it should be &ldquo;nearly impossible&rdquo; for planets to form in binary star systems, it&rsquo;s &ldquo;not surprising&rdquo; that there isn&rsquo;t a straightforward explanation for the existence of planets in such systems; the fact that one potential approach involves &ldquo;complex&rdquo; factors offers further contextual support for this idea. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it would not make sense in context to say that there isn&rsquo;t &ldquo;a discernible&rdquo; explanation&mdash;meaning an explanation capable of being perceived&mdash;for the existence of planets in binary star systems. The text discusses just such an explanation offered by Roman Rafikov and Kedron Silsbee, which indicates that their explanation can be discerned.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the text emphasizes how difficult it is to explain the existence of planets in binary star systems, suggesting that the situation isn&rsquo;t marked by the lack of &ldquo;an inconclusive&rdquo; explanation&mdash;an explanation that does not resolve the issue&mdash;but rather that if any explanations have been offered, they&rsquo;ve likely been inconclusive ones.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that there is a lack of &ldquo;an unbiased,&rdquo; or impartial and unprejudiced, explanation for the existence of planets in binary star systems. The text indicates that it&rsquo;s difficult to explain the existence of planets in such systems and it describes one attempt to do so, but there is no evidence that explanations from Roman Rafikov and Kedron Silsbee or others are biased.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-946-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$cbdca8d4-64aa-4641-b1a5-9e88003bd1c4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5dce6cab","external_id":"bad731cb-eb93-479c-8a7b-c33e8f78e7b0","vaultid":"cb2cde63-3ef8-46d4-8b1a-2e63748e2608","uId":"a2959274-82a2-47ed-82fa-662b158053ef","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Given that the conditions in binary star systems should make planetary formation nearly impossible, it&rsquo;s not surprising that the existence of planets in such systems has lacked <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> explanation. Roman Rafikov and Kedron Silsbee shed light on the subject when they used modeling to determine a complex set of factors that could support planets&rsquo; development.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b8c72143-7d3e-4e19-90cc-95395dd1e7c6","content":"<p>a discernible</p>"},{"id":"a7c26dee-0e90-49c8-acf9-9edeb237813b","content":"<p>a straightforward</p>"},{"id":"0c49ec74-e790-4479-858f-726e6e2584bb","content":"<p>an inconclusive</p>"},{"id":"5b8f2beb-5d25-4cd6-bbe2-cbaf69ed7f02","content":"<p>an unbiased</p>"}],"keys":["a7c26dee-0e90-49c8-acf9-9edeb237813b"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s description of efforts to explain the existence of planets in binary star systems. As used in this context, describing an explanation as &ldquo;a straightforward&rdquo; one would mean that the explanation is direct and uncomplicated. The text asserts that since it should be &ldquo;nearly impossible&rdquo; for planets to form in binary star systems, it&rsquo;s &ldquo;not surprising&rdquo; that there isn&rsquo;t a straightforward explanation for the existence of planets in such systems; the fact that one potential approach involves &ldquo;complex&rdquo; factors offers further contextual support for this idea. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it would not make sense in context to say that there isn&rsquo;t &ldquo;a discernible&rdquo; explanation&mdash;meaning an explanation capable of being perceived&mdash;for the existence of planets in binary star systems. The text discusses just such an explanation offered by Roman Rafikov and Kedron Silsbee, which indicates that their explanation can be discerned.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the text emphasizes how difficult it is to explain the existence of planets in binary star systems, suggesting that the situation isn&rsquo;t marked by the lack of &ldquo;an inconclusive&rdquo; explanation&mdash;an explanation that does not resolve the issue&mdash;but rather that if any explanations have been offered, they&rsquo;ve likely been inconclusive ones.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that there is a lack of &ldquo;an unbiased,&rdquo; or impartial and unprejudiced, explanation for the existence of planets in binary star systems. The text indicates that it&rsquo;s difficult to explain the existence of planets in such systems and it describes one attempt to do so, but there is no evidence that explanations from Roman Rafikov and Kedron Silsbee or others are biased.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-946-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"cbdca8d4-64aa-4641-b1a5-9e88003bd1c4","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959356,"updateDate":1691007959356}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959356, 1691007959356),
    ($SATQ$5e101c70$SATQ$, $SATQ$70210034-540e-47df-82a9-09dd65daf1ae$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$54bbd1c2-d1f7-4028-92d2-074411ae6c6f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level="2" role="heading"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>
<p>Most animals can regenerate some parts of their bodies, such as skin. But when a three-banded panther worm is cut into three pieces, each piece grows into a new worm. Researchers are investigating this feat partly to learn more about humans&rsquo; comparatively limited abilities to regenerate, and they&rsquo;re making exciting progress. An especially promising discovery is that both humans and panther worms have a gene for early growth response (EGR) linked to regeneration.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level="2" role="heading"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>When Mansi Srivastava and her team reported that panther worms, like humans, possess a gene for EGR, it caused excitement. However, as the team pointed out, the gene likely functions very differently in humans than it does in panther worms. Srivastava has likened EGR to a switch that activates other genes involved in regeneration in panther worms, but how this switch operates in humans remains unclear.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, what would the author of Text 2 most likely say about Text 1&rsquo;s characterization of the discovery involving EGR?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c0657c51-13df-43b8-a098-e4c908c96015","content":"<p>It is reasonable given that Srivastava and her team have identified how EGR functions in both humans and panther worms.</p>"},{"id":"36aadb98-c1c0-45aa-8086-d9f3d9f5704e","content":"<p>It is overly optimistic given additional observations from Srivastava and her team.</p>"},{"id":"8c4955e5-e01b-48c3-9068-967b8e499852","content":"<p>It is unexpected given that Srivastava and her team&rsquo;s findings were generally met with enthusiasm.</p>"},{"id":"60356f1c-029c-4179-ba43-de2ace59a486","content":"<p>It is unfairly dismissive given the progress that Srivastava and her team have reported.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["36aadb98-c1c0-45aa-8086-d9f3d9f5704e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it reflects how the author of Text 2 would most likely respond to Text 1 based on the information provided. Text 1 discusses the discovery of a regeneration-linked gene, EGR, in both three-banded panther worms (which are capable of full regeneration) and humans (who have relatively limited regeneration abilities). Text 1 characterizes this discovery as &ldquo;especially promising&rdquo; and a sign of &ldquo;exciting progress&rdquo; in understanding human regeneration. The author of Text 2, on the other hand, focuses on the fact that the team that reported the EGR finding pointed out that while EGR&rsquo;s function in humans isn&rsquo;t yet known, it&rsquo;s likely very different from its function in panther worms. Therefore, the author of Text 2 would most likely say that Text 1&rsquo;s enthusiasm about the EGR discovery is overly optimistic given Srivastava&rsquo;s team&rsquo;s observations about EGR in humans. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the author of Text 2 explains that Srivastava and her team explicitly reported that they haven&rsquo;t yet identified how EGR functions in humans; therefore, the author of Text 2 wouldn&rsquo;t say that Text 1&rsquo;s excitement is reasonable for the stated reason. Instead, the author of Text 2 would likely characterize Text 1&rsquo;s excitement as premature and overly optimistic. Choice C is incorrect because Text 1 does treat Srivastava&rsquo;s team&rsquo;s findings with enthusiasm; it describes the discovery of EGR in both three-banded panther worms and humans as promising and exciting. It would be illogical for the author of Text 2 to say that because most others treat the discovery with enthusiasm, Text 1&rsquo;s enthusiastic characterization of the discovery is unexpected. Choice D is incorrect because Text 1 isn&rsquo;t at all dismissive of Srivastava&rsquo;s team&rsquo;s findings; instead, Text 1 is optimistic about the EGR discovery, characterizing it as promising and exciting. There&rsquo;s nothing in Text 2 to suggest that the author of Text 2 would say that Text 1&rsquo;s praise for the discovery is dismissive, or disdainful.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-007-CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b96feec8-e826-464b-badb-2e105730190d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5e101c70","external_id":"70210034-540e-47df-82a9-09dd65daf1ae","vaultid":"54bbd1c2-d1f7-4028-92d2-074411ae6c6f","uId":"a4a359c5-eeed-4604-a702-6f3a8d52e75e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level=\"2\" role=\"heading\"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>\n<p>Most animals can regenerate some parts of their bodies, such as skin. But when a three-banded panther worm is cut into three pieces, each piece grows into a new worm. Researchers are investigating this feat partly to learn more about humans&rsquo; comparatively limited abilities to regenerate, and they&rsquo;re making exciting progress. An especially promising discovery is that both humans and panther worms have a gene for early growth response (EGR) linked to regeneration.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level=\"2\" role=\"heading\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>When Mansi Srivastava and her team reported that panther worms, like humans, possess a gene for EGR, it caused excitement. However, as the team pointed out, the gene likely functions very differently in humans than it does in panther worms. Srivastava has likened EGR to a switch that activates other genes involved in regeneration in panther worms, but how this switch operates in humans remains unclear.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, what would the author of Text 2 most likely say about Text 1&rsquo;s characterization of the discovery involving EGR?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c0657c51-13df-43b8-a098-e4c908c96015","content":"<p>It is reasonable given that Srivastava and her team have identified how EGR functions in both humans and panther worms.</p>"},{"id":"36aadb98-c1c0-45aa-8086-d9f3d9f5704e","content":"<p>It is overly optimistic given additional observations from Srivastava and her team.</p>"},{"id":"8c4955e5-e01b-48c3-9068-967b8e499852","content":"<p>It is unexpected given that Srivastava and her team&rsquo;s findings were generally met with enthusiasm.</p>"},{"id":"60356f1c-029c-4179-ba43-de2ace59a486","content":"<p>It is unfairly dismissive given the progress that Srivastava and her team have reported.</p>"}],"keys":["36aadb98-c1c0-45aa-8086-d9f3d9f5704e"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it reflects how the author of Text 2 would most likely respond to Text 1 based on the information provided. Text 1 discusses the discovery of a regeneration-linked gene, EGR, in both three-banded panther worms (which are capable of full regeneration) and humans (who have relatively limited regeneration abilities). Text 1 characterizes this discovery as &ldquo;especially promising&rdquo; and a sign of &ldquo;exciting progress&rdquo; in understanding human regeneration. The author of Text 2, on the other hand, focuses on the fact that the team that reported the EGR finding pointed out that while EGR&rsquo;s function in humans isn&rsquo;t yet known, it&rsquo;s likely very different from its function in panther worms. Therefore, the author of Text 2 would most likely say that Text 1&rsquo;s enthusiasm about the EGR discovery is overly optimistic given Srivastava&rsquo;s team&rsquo;s observations about EGR in humans. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the author of Text 2 explains that Srivastava and her team explicitly reported that they haven&rsquo;t yet identified how EGR functions in humans; therefore, the author of Text 2 wouldn&rsquo;t say that Text 1&rsquo;s excitement is reasonable for the stated reason. Instead, the author of Text 2 would likely characterize Text 1&rsquo;s excitement as premature and overly optimistic. Choice C is incorrect because Text 1 does treat Srivastava&rsquo;s team&rsquo;s findings with enthusiasm; it describes the discovery of EGR in both three-banded panther worms and humans as promising and exciting. It would be illogical for the author of Text 2 to say that because most others treat the discovery with enthusiasm, Text 1&rsquo;s enthusiastic characterization of the discovery is unexpected. Choice D is incorrect because Text 1 isn&rsquo;t at all dismissive of Srivastava&rsquo;s team&rsquo;s findings; instead, Text 1 is optimistic about the EGR discovery, characterizing it as promising and exciting. There&rsquo;s nothing in Text 2 to suggest that the author of Text 2 would say that Text 1&rsquo;s praise for the discovery is dismissive, or disdainful.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-007-CTC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b96feec8-e826-464b-badb-2e105730190d","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959313,"updateDate":1691007959313}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959313, 1691007959313),
    ($SATQ$5e4e082c$SATQ$, $SATQ$5fe644fc-ee4d-4803-a075-bee28870961b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><span class="paired_passage" role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>
<p>Chemically analyzing bulk rock-core samples from Australia&rsquo;s Mount McRae Shale, Ariel Anbar <span data-ssml-sub-alias="et al">et al.</span> found a transient but significant increase in molybdenum (<span data-ssml-say-as="characters">Mo</span>) at a point corresponding to roughly 2.5 billion years ago (<span data-ssml-say-as="characters">Ga</span>). On Earth, <span data-ssml-say-as="characters">Mo</span> is released mainly through oxidative weathering of minerals; Anbar <span data-ssml-sub-alias="et al">et al.</span> therefore concluded that atmospheric oxygen briefly increased 2.5 <span data-ssml-say-as="characters">Ga</span>, then returned to its earlier negligible level.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span class="paired_passage" role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>Sarah Slotznick <span data-ssml-sub-alias="et al">et al.</span> reexamined the Mount McRae Shale core. Since chemically analyzing bulk samples can occlude contextual details, Slotznick <span data-ssml-sub-alias="et al">et al.</span> also employed high-resolution microscopy, which revealed volcanic debris&mdash;a known <span data-ssml-say-as="characters">Mo</span> host&mdash;around 2.5 <span data-ssml-say-as="characters">Ga</span> and microfractures in the surrounding matrix. The researchers assert that fluid could have reached the debris through the microfractures and initiated oxidative weathering long after debris deposition.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, Anbar <span data-ssml-sub-alias="et al">et al.</span> (Text 1) and Slotznick <span data-ssml-sub-alias="et al">et al.</span> (Text 2) would most likely disagree about the answer to which question about the portion of the Mount McRae Shale rock-core corresponding to 2.5 <span data-ssml-say-as="characters">Ga</span>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"734fbc27-eefa-4ac6-8647-5cd301553994","content":"<p>Does the increase in M<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">o</span> in that portion suggest that atmospheric oxygen levels were not negligible before 2.5 <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ga</span>?</p>"},{"id":"eb87edbc-796b-4f88-b76d-e58153f5d46c","content":"<p>Is the increase in <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Mo</span> in that portion indicative of an increase in atmospheric oxygen dating to the same time?</p>"},{"id":"22a7559f-5e15-45c8-b6f8-06b06339634f","content":"<p>Is the increase in M<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">o</span> in that portion attributable to oxidative weathering of the material found in that portion?</p>"},{"id":"a24bd789-46b7-4f2e-bdc8-eb9899ac5501","content":"<p>Did chemically analyzing bulk samples lead to a false impression that there is an increase in <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Mo</span> in that portion?</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["eb87edbc-796b-4f88-b76d-e58153f5d46c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because Anbar et al. and Slotznick et al. would most likely disagree about whether the molybdenum (Mo) increase indicates atmospheric oxygen levels specifically at 2.5 Ga. Anbar et al. conclude that the Mo increase they found &ldquo;at a point corresponding to roughly 2.5 billion years ago&rdquo; means that &ldquo;atmospheric oxygen briefly increased 2.5 Ga, then returned to its earlier negligible level,&rdquo; directly linking the timing of the Mo increase to contemporaneous atmospheric oxygen levels. In contrast, Slotznick et al. discovered volcanic debris and microfractures in the rock core and &ldquo;assert that fluid could have reached the debris through the microfractures and initiated oxidative weathering long after debris deposition.&rdquo; If, as Slotznick asserts, the Mo detected at the 2.5 Ga portion could have been deposited there much later through weathering processes, then the Mo increase wouldn&rsquo;t necessarily indicate atmospheric oxygen levels at 2.5 Ga specifically. Thus, Anbar et al. and Slotznick et al. disagree on whether the increase in Mo in the portion of the rock core corresponding to 2.5 Ga is indicative of an increase in atmospheric oxygen dating to the same time.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because neither research team&rsquo;s findings directly address atmospheric oxygen levels before 2.5 Ga. Anbar et al. conclude that oxygen &ldquo;returned to its earlier negligible level&rdquo; after 2.5 Ga, implying low pre&ndash;2.5 Ga levels, while Slotznick et al. focus on explaining the Mo increase through later weathering processes rather than making claims about earlier atmospheric conditions. Choice C is incorrect because both research teams would likely agree that the Mo increase is attributable to oxidative weathering. Anbar et al. state that &ldquo;Mo is released mainly through oxidative weathering of minerals,&rdquo; and Slotznick et al. also reference &ldquo;oxidative weathering&rdquo; as the process that released Mo from the volcanic debris. Their disagreement concerns the timing of this weathering, not whether oxidative weathering is responsible. Choice D is incorrect because Slotznick et al. don&rsquo;t suggest that bulk chemical analysis created a false impression about the presence of increased Mo. They acknowledge that the Mo increase exists but offer a different explanation for its timing and source. They critique bulk analysis for occluding &ldquo;contextual details&rdquo; that would require another kind of analysis to uncover, not for producing inaccurate measurements of Mo levels.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5e4e082c","external_id":"5fe644fc-ee4d-4803-a075-bee28870961b","vaultid":null,"uId":"978a0ffb-157d-449a-bbcd-1782eacd41c5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><span class=\"paired_passage\" role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>\n<p>Chemically analyzing bulk rock-core samples from Australia&rsquo;s Mount McRae Shale, Ariel Anbar <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> found a transient but significant increase in molybdenum (<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Mo</span>) at a point corresponding to roughly 2.5 billion years ago (<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ga</span>). On Earth, <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Mo</span> is released mainly through oxidative weathering of minerals; Anbar <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> therefore concluded that atmospheric oxygen briefly increased 2.5 <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ga</span>, then returned to its earlier negligible level.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><span class=\"paired_passage\" role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>Sarah Slotznick <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> reexamined the Mount McRae Shale core. Since chemically analyzing bulk samples can occlude contextual details, Slotznick <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> also employed high-resolution microscopy, which revealed volcanic debris&mdash;a known <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Mo</span> host&mdash;around 2.5 <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ga</span> and microfractures in the surrounding matrix. The researchers assert that fluid could have reached the debris through the microfractures and initiated oxidative weathering long after debris deposition.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, Anbar <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> (Text 1) and Slotznick <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> (Text 2) would most likely disagree about the answer to which question about the portion of the Mount McRae Shale rock-core corresponding to 2.5 <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ga</span>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"734fbc27-eefa-4ac6-8647-5cd301553994","content":"<p>Does the increase in M<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">o</span> in that portion suggest that atmospheric oxygen levels were not negligible before 2.5 <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ga</span>?</p>"},{"id":"eb87edbc-796b-4f88-b76d-e58153f5d46c","content":"<p>Is the increase in <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Mo</span> in that portion indicative of an increase in atmospheric oxygen dating to the same time?</p>"},{"id":"22a7559f-5e15-45c8-b6f8-06b06339634f","content":"<p>Is the increase in M<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">o</span> in that portion attributable to oxidative weathering of the material found in that portion?</p>"},{"id":"a24bd789-46b7-4f2e-bdc8-eb9899ac5501","content":"<p>Did chemically analyzing bulk samples lead to a false impression that there is an increase in <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Mo</span> in that portion?</p>"}],"keys":["eb87edbc-796b-4f88-b76d-e58153f5d46c"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because Anbar et al. and Slotznick et al. would most likely disagree about whether the molybdenum (Mo) increase indicates atmospheric oxygen levels specifically at 2.5 Ga. Anbar et al. conclude that the Mo increase they found &ldquo;at a point corresponding to roughly 2.5 billion years ago&rdquo; means that &ldquo;atmospheric oxygen briefly increased 2.5 Ga, then returned to its earlier negligible level,&rdquo; directly linking the timing of the Mo increase to contemporaneous atmospheric oxygen levels. In contrast, Slotznick et al. discovered volcanic debris and microfractures in the rock core and &ldquo;assert that fluid could have reached the debris through the microfractures and initiated oxidative weathering long after debris deposition.&rdquo; If, as Slotznick asserts, the Mo detected at the 2.5 Ga portion could have been deposited there much later through weathering processes, then the Mo increase wouldn&rsquo;t necessarily indicate atmospheric oxygen levels at 2.5 Ga specifically. Thus, Anbar et al. and Slotznick et al. disagree on whether the increase in Mo in the portion of the rock core corresponding to 2.5 Ga is indicative of an increase in atmospheric oxygen dating to the same time.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because neither research team&rsquo;s findings directly address atmospheric oxygen levels before 2.5 Ga. Anbar et al. conclude that oxygen &ldquo;returned to its earlier negligible level&rdquo; after 2.5 Ga, implying low pre&ndash;2.5 Ga levels, while Slotznick et al. focus on explaining the Mo increase through later weathering processes rather than making claims about earlier atmospheric conditions. Choice C is incorrect because both research teams would likely agree that the Mo increase is attributable to oxidative weathering. Anbar et al. state that &ldquo;Mo is released mainly through oxidative weathering of minerals,&rdquo; and Slotznick et al. also reference &ldquo;oxidative weathering&rdquo; as the process that released Mo from the volcanic debris. Their disagreement concerns the timing of this weathering, not whether oxidative weathering is responsible. Choice D is incorrect because Slotznick et al. don&rsquo;t suggest that bulk chemical analysis created a false impression about the presence of increased Mo. They acknowledge that the Mo increase exists but offer a different explanation for its timing and source. They critique bulk analysis for occluding &ldquo;contextual details&rdquo; that would require another kind of analysis to uncover, not for producing inaccurate measurements of Mo levels.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$5e57efec$SATQ$, $SATQ$845daa86-f3a1-4235-a02a-508a08f01b9f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$fd4bd2df-1f1b-4200-9c94-4a1f4822bc54$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Economist Marco Castillo and colleagues showed that nuisance costs&mdash;the time and effort people must spend to make donations&mdash;reduce charitable giving. Charities can mitigate this effect by compensating donors for nuisance costs, but those costs, though variable, are largely <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> donation size, so charities that compensate donors will likely favor attracting a few large donors over many small donors.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5186678c-1c96-4849-89df-6b378ca55f2b","content":"<p>supplemental to</p>"},{"id":"92b95382-b377-4348-ac28-239a606bf9d0","content":"<p>predictive of</p>"},{"id":"1a980523-79c1-45de-a2d0-2aef2617a91b","content":"<p>independent of</p>"},{"id":"929e0c95-cd88-494e-a175-94d27c81c9bb","content":"<p>subsumed in</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1a980523-79c1-45de-a2d0-2aef2617a91b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. We&rsquo;re told that charities that pay donors back for nuisance costs will attract a few large donors instead of many small donors. This suggests that nuisance costs are not linked to donation size. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t fit the logic of the text. If nuisance costs are supplemental to (meaning in addition to) donation size, that wouldn&rsquo;t result in charities that compensate donors for those costs attracting a few large donors over many small donors. Choice B is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t fit the logic of the text. If nuisance costs can predict donation size, that wouldn&rsquo;t necessarily result in charities that compensate donors for those costs attracting a few large donors over many small donors. Choice D is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t fit the logic of the text. If nuisance costs are subsumed in (meaning included in) donation size, that wouldn&rsquo;t result in charities that compensate donors for those costs attracting a few large donors over many small donors. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-H003$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e64635ec-fb7a-45e2-80c1-9abe6ba95f98$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5e57efec","external_id":"845daa86-f3a1-4235-a02a-508a08f01b9f","vaultid":"fd4bd2df-1f1b-4200-9c94-4a1f4822bc54","uId":"07e35436-da6d-41c0-b0ab-38d29ebec821","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Economist Marco Castillo and colleagues showed that nuisance costs&mdash;the time and effort people must spend to make donations&mdash;reduce charitable giving. Charities can mitigate this effect by compensating donors for nuisance costs, but those costs, though variable, are largely <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> donation size, so charities that compensate donors will likely favor attracting a few large donors over many small donors.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5186678c-1c96-4849-89df-6b378ca55f2b","content":"<p>supplemental to</p>"},{"id":"92b95382-b377-4348-ac28-239a606bf9d0","content":"<p>predictive of</p>"},{"id":"1a980523-79c1-45de-a2d0-2aef2617a91b","content":"<p>independent of</p>"},{"id":"929e0c95-cd88-494e-a175-94d27c81c9bb","content":"<p>subsumed in</p>"}],"keys":["1a980523-79c1-45de-a2d0-2aef2617a91b"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. We&rsquo;re told that charities that pay donors back for nuisance costs will attract a few large donors instead of many small donors. This suggests that nuisance costs are not linked to donation size. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t fit the logic of the text. If nuisance costs are supplemental to (meaning in addition to) donation size, that wouldn&rsquo;t result in charities that compensate donors for those costs attracting a few large donors over many small donors. Choice B is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t fit the logic of the text. If nuisance costs can predict donation size, that wouldn&rsquo;t necessarily result in charities that compensate donors for those costs attracting a few large donors over many small donors. Choice D is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t fit the logic of the text. If nuisance costs are subsumed in (meaning included in) donation size, that wouldn&rsquo;t result in charities that compensate donors for those costs attracting a few large donors over many small donors. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-H003","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e64635ec-fb7a-45e2-80c1-9abe6ba95f98","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$5e732e67$SATQ$, $SATQ$c108292b-f5d4-48c1-8551-791dae982b3e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$1723f7ea-1754-4ab0-83c7-5fb66957ef00$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Many films from the early 1900s have been lost. These losses include several films by the first wave of Black women filmmakers. We know about these lost movies only from small pieces of evidence. For example, an advertisement for Jennie Louise Touissant Welcome&rsquo;s documentary <em>Doing Their Bit</em> still exists. There&rsquo;s a reference in a magazine to Tressie Souders&rsquo;s film <em>A Woman&rsquo;s Error</em>. And Maria P. Williams&rsquo;s <em>The Flames of Wrath</em> is mentioned in a letter and a newspaper article, and one image from the movie was discovered in the 1990s.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"72e0c490-e1ad-4984-95fe-c2b63be6eb18","content":"<p>The text identifies a complex problem, then presents examples of unsuccessful attempts to solve that problem.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"afa6829d-a040-469e-bfb9-068723e8bc3f","content":"<p>The text summarizes a debate among researchers, then gives reasons for supporting one side in that debate.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"b979062a-3de3-4d5f-a472-9af3c09ab760","content":"<p>The text describes a general situation, then illustrates that situation with specific examples.</p>"},{"id":"2dfabf52-8b4e-4ba8-be6f-cfd38f91057c","content":"<p>The text discusses several notable individuals, then explains commonly overlooked differences between those individuals.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b979062a-3de3-4d5f-a472-9af3c09ab760"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The first three sentences describe the general situation: these early films have been lost, and we only know about them from small pieces of evidence. The rest of the text offers specific examples of the small pieces of evidence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the overall structure. The fact that we only know about these lost early films from small pieces of evidence isn&rsquo;t presented as a &ldquo;complex problem&rdquo;&mdash;that&rsquo;s too extreme. And the examples presented are not &ldquo;unsuccessful attempts&rdquo; to solve it. If anything, the examples represent a success, because we discovered that these films existed in the first place. Choice B is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the overall structure. There&rsquo;s no &ldquo;debate&rdquo; presented in the text, so there&rsquo;s no &ldquo;side&rdquo; for the text to support. Choice D is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the overall structure. The text doesn&rsquo;t discuss any &ldquo;differences&rdquo; between the filmmakers. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TSP-E002$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$4222602a-0372-44d2-99f1-db571b3bf714$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5e732e67","external_id":"c108292b-f5d4-48c1-8551-791dae982b3e","vaultid":"1723f7ea-1754-4ab0-83c7-5fb66957ef00","uId":"c4195587-80c5-4da8-a4fb-f1de1e93c448","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Many films from the early 1900s have been lost. These losses include several films by the first wave of Black women filmmakers. We know about these lost movies only from small pieces of evidence. For example, an advertisement for Jennie Louise Touissant Welcome&rsquo;s documentary <em>Doing Their Bit</em> still exists. There&rsquo;s a reference in a magazine to Tressie Souders&rsquo;s film <em>A Woman&rsquo;s Error</em>. And Maria P. Williams&rsquo;s <em>The Flames of Wrath</em> is mentioned in a letter and a newspaper article, and one image from the movie was discovered in the 1990s.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"72e0c490-e1ad-4984-95fe-c2b63be6eb18","content":"<p>The text identifies a complex problem, then presents examples of unsuccessful attempts to solve that problem.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"afa6829d-a040-469e-bfb9-068723e8bc3f","content":"<p>The text summarizes a debate among researchers, then gives reasons for supporting one side in that debate.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"b979062a-3de3-4d5f-a472-9af3c09ab760","content":"<p>The text describes a general situation, then illustrates that situation with specific examples.</p>"},{"id":"2dfabf52-8b4e-4ba8-be6f-cfd38f91057c","content":"<p>The text discusses several notable individuals, then explains commonly overlooked differences between those individuals.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["b979062a-3de3-4d5f-a472-9af3c09ab760"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The first three sentences describe the general situation: these early films have been lost, and we only know about them from small pieces of evidence. The rest of the text offers specific examples of the small pieces of evidence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the overall structure. The fact that we only know about these lost early films from small pieces of evidence isn&rsquo;t presented as a &ldquo;complex problem&rdquo;&mdash;that&rsquo;s too extreme. And the examples presented are not &ldquo;unsuccessful attempts&rdquo; to solve it. If anything, the examples represent a success, because we discovered that these films existed in the first place. Choice B is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the overall structure. There&rsquo;s no &ldquo;debate&rdquo; presented in the text, so there&rsquo;s no &ldquo;side&rdquo; for the text to support. Choice D is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the overall structure. The text doesn&rsquo;t discuss any &ldquo;differences&rdquo; between the filmmakers. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TSP-E002","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"4222602a-0372-44d2-99f1-db571b3bf714","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959374,"updateDate":1691007959374}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959374, 1691007959374),
    ($SATQ$5effa190$SATQ$, $SATQ$5322a747-7ecf-48ec-a8dc-eead867b57de$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ffcab899-a813-485b-b0c3-77f2c2292472$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The process of mechanically recycling plastics is often considered <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> because of the environmental impact and the loss of material quality that often occurs. But chemist Takunda Chazovachii has helped develop a cleaner process of chemical recycling that converts superabsorbent polymers from diapers into a desirable reusable adhesive.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"29a420d2-7f63-4324-980b-a3ce266bd962","content":"<p>resilient</p>"},{"id":"42a4f81e-1436-4a29-9b05-137ffc7c1fb2","content":"<p>inadequate</p>"},{"id":"e88c569d-4c81-4127-9a35-8053e46bce7c","content":"<p>dynamic</p>"},{"id":"05969ce2-c042-4059-bf68-835b9170d2d7","content":"<p>satisfactory</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["42a4f81e-1436-4a29-9b05-137ffc7c1fb2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion about recycling plastics. In this context, &ldquo;inadequate&rdquo; means not satisfactory. The text indicates that the mechanical plastic-recycling process affects the environment and causes &ldquo;the loss of material quality.&rdquo; The text contrasts that with Chazovachii&rsquo;s chemical plastic-recycling process, which is cleaner and produces a desirable product. The text&rsquo;s emphasis on the negative aspects of mechanical recycling suggests that it is inadequate in terms of environmental impact and the quality of the material the process yields. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;resilient&rdquo; would mean able to withstand difficulty and the text does not characterize the plastic-recycling process as having this quality or describe any difficulties that these processes might need to overcome. Choice C is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;dynamic&rdquo; would mean constantly changing. Although the text suggests that there have been changes in the field of recycling, as is the case with the advent of Chazovachii&rsquo;s chemical recycling process, there is nothing to suggest that the mechanical process itself has changed or is prone to change. Choice D is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;satisfactory&rdquo; would mean acceptable but not perfect. The text mentions only shortcomings of the mechanical process (environmental effects and lower material quality), so the text more strongly supports a negative view of this process and provides no evidence that it would be considered satisfactory. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-932-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$25befa61-fe88-42ba-a666-31ea16287c9c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5effa190","external_id":"5322a747-7ecf-48ec-a8dc-eead867b57de","vaultid":"ffcab899-a813-485b-b0c3-77f2c2292472","uId":"2f54577a-15f4-479b-ae98-660969a14472","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The process of mechanically recycling plastics is often considered <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> because of the environmental impact and the loss of material quality that often occurs. But chemist Takunda Chazovachii has helped develop a cleaner process of chemical recycling that converts superabsorbent polymers from diapers into a desirable reusable adhesive.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"29a420d2-7f63-4324-980b-a3ce266bd962","content":"<p>resilient</p>"},{"id":"42a4f81e-1436-4a29-9b05-137ffc7c1fb2","content":"<p>inadequate</p>"},{"id":"e88c569d-4c81-4127-9a35-8053e46bce7c","content":"<p>dynamic</p>"},{"id":"05969ce2-c042-4059-bf68-835b9170d2d7","content":"<p>satisfactory</p>"}],"keys":["42a4f81e-1436-4a29-9b05-137ffc7c1fb2"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion about recycling plastics. In this context, &ldquo;inadequate&rdquo; means not satisfactory. The text indicates that the mechanical plastic-recycling process affects the environment and causes &ldquo;the loss of material quality.&rdquo; The text contrasts that with Chazovachii&rsquo;s chemical plastic-recycling process, which is cleaner and produces a desirable product. The text&rsquo;s emphasis on the negative aspects of mechanical recycling suggests that it is inadequate in terms of environmental impact and the quality of the material the process yields. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;resilient&rdquo; would mean able to withstand difficulty and the text does not characterize the plastic-recycling process as having this quality or describe any difficulties that these processes might need to overcome. Choice C is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;dynamic&rdquo; would mean constantly changing. Although the text suggests that there have been changes in the field of recycling, as is the case with the advent of Chazovachii&rsquo;s chemical recycling process, there is nothing to suggest that the mechanical process itself has changed or is prone to change. Choice D is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;satisfactory&rdquo; would mean acceptable but not perfect. The text mentions only shortcomings of the mechanical process (environmental effects and lower material quality), so the text more strongly supports a negative view of this process and provides no evidence that it would be considered satisfactory. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-932-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"25befa61-fe88-42ba-a666-31ea16287c9c","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959356,"updateDate":1691007959356}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959356, 1691007959356),
    ($SATQ$5f56fdec$SATQ$, $SATQ$c34ddb49-ca35-4b85-b758-a1019984c4d2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from George Marion McClellan&rsquo;s 1895 poem &ldquo;Eternity.&rdquo;</p>
<blockquote class="dap_poemexcerpt">
<p>My spirit swoons, and all my senses cry</p>
<p>For Ocean&rsquo;s breast and covering of the sky.</p>
<p>Rock me to sleep, ye waves, and outward bound,</p>
<p>Just let me drift far out from toil and care,</p>
<p>Where lapping of the waves shall be the sound,</p>
<p>Which mingled with the winds that gently bear</p>
<p>Me on between a peaceful sea and sky,</p>
<p>To make my soothing slumberous lullaby.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0567e9a0-99e1-48ff-9f0f-14db5465fba8","content":"<p>To illustrate the increasing intensity of the speaker&rsquo;s desire to escape ongoing hardship by gliding on the ocean</p>"},{"id":"594034eb-8b95-495d-8acd-f6908ac0d542","content":"<p>To contrast the demands of the speaker&rsquo;s everyday life with the serenity of being rocked to sleep by the ocean&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"c1ca0786-5330-4e00-b394-7970b09c4470","content":"<p>To convey the speaker&rsquo;s longing for the ocean to impart a sense of inner tranquility</p>"},{"id":"d48d087e-53ef-48a3-8a93-cff24ff3241e","content":"<p>To justify the speaker&rsquo;s qualms about being transported by the ocean to a quiet destination</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c1ca0786-5330-4e00-b394-7970b09c4470"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately states the main purpose of the text, which is to convey the speaker&rsquo;s longing for the ocean to impart a sense of inner tranquility, or peace. The text begins by expressing the speaker&rsquo;s strong desire to be on the ocean, appealing directly to the waves to rock the speaker to sleep. The poem then describes the "slumberous lullaby" formed by the sound of the waves and the winds on which the speaker wishes to be carried "gently...between a peaceful sea and sky." All these details serve to emphasize the speaker&rsquo;s desire for the ocean to give them a sense of inner tranquility.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Given the poem&rsquo;s expression of a longing to achieve tranquility by drifting on the ocean, it can be inferred that the speaker desires to escape something unpleasant in daily life. However, the poem doesn&rsquo;t refer directly to any such hardship&mdash;ongoing or otherwise. And since the poem doesn&rsquo;t establish that the speaker is experiencing a desire to escape ongoing hardship, it can&rsquo;t be said that such a desire increases in intensity. Choice B is incorrect. The speaker does express a desire for the serenity of being rocked to sleep by the ocean, and it can be inferred that this desire is a response to demands that make the speaker&rsquo;s daily life stressful. However, the poem doesn&rsquo;t refer directly to such demands or contrast them with the tranquility of drifting on the ocean. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest the speaker has any qualms, or reservations, about being transported by the ocean. On the contrary, the speaker actively desires to "drift far out" and be carried by the waves and wind. Moreover, the poem doesn&rsquo;t suggest that this drifting would have any particular destination.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5f56fdec","external_id":"c34ddb49-ca35-4b85-b758-a1019984c4d2","vaultid":null,"uId":"19e7a039-c56e-47d2-b158-e21a8308c847","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from George Marion McClellan&rsquo;s 1895 poem &ldquo;Eternity.&rdquo;</p>\n<blockquote class=\"dap_poemexcerpt\">\n<p>My spirit swoons, and all my senses cry</p>\n<p>For Ocean&rsquo;s breast and covering of the sky.</p>\n<p>Rock me to sleep, ye waves, and outward bound,</p>\n<p>Just let me drift far out from toil and care,</p>\n<p>Where lapping of the waves shall be the sound,</p>\n<p>Which mingled with the winds that gently bear</p>\n<p>Me on between a peaceful sea and sky,</p>\n<p>To make my soothing slumberous lullaby.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0567e9a0-99e1-48ff-9f0f-14db5465fba8","content":"<p>To illustrate the increasing intensity of the speaker&rsquo;s desire to escape ongoing hardship by gliding on the ocean</p>"},{"id":"594034eb-8b95-495d-8acd-f6908ac0d542","content":"<p>To contrast the demands of the speaker&rsquo;s everyday life with the serenity of being rocked to sleep by the ocean&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"c1ca0786-5330-4e00-b394-7970b09c4470","content":"<p>To convey the speaker&rsquo;s longing for the ocean to impart a sense of inner tranquility</p>"},{"id":"d48d087e-53ef-48a3-8a93-cff24ff3241e","content":"<p>To justify the speaker&rsquo;s qualms about being transported by the ocean to a quiet destination</p>"}],"keys":["c1ca0786-5330-4e00-b394-7970b09c4470"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately states the main purpose of the text, which is to convey the speaker&rsquo;s longing for the ocean to impart a sense of inner tranquility, or peace. The text begins by expressing the speaker&rsquo;s strong desire to be on the ocean, appealing directly to the waves to rock the speaker to sleep. The poem then describes the \"slumberous lullaby\" formed by the sound of the waves and the winds on which the speaker wishes to be carried \"gently...between a peaceful sea and sky.\" All these details serve to emphasize the speaker&rsquo;s desire for the ocean to give them a sense of inner tranquility.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Given the poem&rsquo;s expression of a longing to achieve tranquility by drifting on the ocean, it can be inferred that the speaker desires to escape something unpleasant in daily life. However, the poem doesn&rsquo;t refer directly to any such hardship&mdash;ongoing or otherwise. And since the poem doesn&rsquo;t establish that the speaker is experiencing a desire to escape ongoing hardship, it can&rsquo;t be said that such a desire increases in intensity. Choice B is incorrect. The speaker does express a desire for the serenity of being rocked to sleep by the ocean, and it can be inferred that this desire is a response to demands that make the speaker&rsquo;s daily life stressful. However, the poem doesn&rsquo;t refer directly to such demands or contrast them with the tranquility of drifting on the ocean. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest the speaker has any qualms, or reservations, about being transported by the ocean. On the contrary, the speaker actively desires to \"drift far out\" and be carried by the waves and wind. Moreover, the poem doesn&rsquo;t suggest that this drifting would have any particular destination.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061191,"updateDate":1755117061191}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061191, 1755117061191),
    ($SATQ$5fa165f7$SATQ$, $SATQ$3eafd2cd-6318-41d8-90b3-f2a3b05f70a4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e903f7c7-3231-4914-8c2d-f37ce364089f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In the 1960s, Sam Gilliam, a Black painter from the southern United States, became the first artist to drape painted canvases into flowing shapes. He later explored a different style, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> quilt-like paintings inspired by the patchwork quilting tradition of Black communities in the South.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"956a0ff4-14a3-4106-acaa-46de441a75fd","content":"<p>predicting</p>"},{"id":"44f47ed3-22ec-4f8d-bff4-aa5792b759be","content":"<p>refusing</p>"},{"id":"af8ab3e6-dd3c-4807-a951-d8631d12688f","content":"<p>hiding</p>"},{"id":"65abb54e-e824-4e3a-8ec9-bca64f4cc9a3","content":"<p>creating</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["65abb54e-e824-4e3a-8ec9-bca64f4cc9a3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Sam Gilliam&rsquo;s artworks. As used in this context, &ldquo;creating&rdquo; means producing or bringing something into existence. The text indicates that Gilliam is an artist who made draped canvases and, later, quilt-like paintings. This context supports the idea that Gilliam explored different styles in his art by creating special types of paintings. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text indicates that Gilliam actually explored and pursued the creation of quilt-like paintings; he wasn&rsquo;t just &ldquo;predicting,&rdquo; or declaring in advance, the existence of these paintings. Choice B is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;refusing&rdquo; would mean rejecting, and there is nothing in the text to suggest that Gilliam rejected his quilt-like paintings. Instead, the text indicates that he was exploring and pursuing a new art style in these paintings. Choice C is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;hiding&rdquo; would mean concealing from view, and there is nothing in the text to suggest that Gilliam attempted to conceal his quilt-like paintings. Instead, the text indicates that he was exploring and pursuing a new art style in these paintings.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-056-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$6f7e8e5b-d3a7-47cb-afdf-42f6736727a5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5fa165f7","external_id":"3eafd2cd-6318-41d8-90b3-f2a3b05f70a4","vaultid":"e903f7c7-3231-4914-8c2d-f37ce364089f","uId":"ec08269b-96a9-4f9b-846f-46d90a31ccd4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In the 1960s, Sam Gilliam, a Black painter from the southern United States, became the first artist to drape painted canvases into flowing shapes. He later explored a different style, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> quilt-like paintings inspired by the patchwork quilting tradition of Black communities in the South.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"956a0ff4-14a3-4106-acaa-46de441a75fd","content":"<p>predicting</p>"},{"id":"44f47ed3-22ec-4f8d-bff4-aa5792b759be","content":"<p>refusing</p>"},{"id":"af8ab3e6-dd3c-4807-a951-d8631d12688f","content":"<p>hiding</p>"},{"id":"65abb54e-e824-4e3a-8ec9-bca64f4cc9a3","content":"<p>creating</p>"}],"keys":["65abb54e-e824-4e3a-8ec9-bca64f4cc9a3"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Sam Gilliam&rsquo;s artworks. As used in this context, &ldquo;creating&rdquo; means producing or bringing something into existence. The text indicates that Gilliam is an artist who made draped canvases and, later, quilt-like paintings. This context supports the idea that Gilliam explored different styles in his art by creating special types of paintings. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text indicates that Gilliam actually explored and pursued the creation of quilt-like paintings; he wasn&rsquo;t just &ldquo;predicting,&rdquo; or declaring in advance, the existence of these paintings. Choice B is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;refusing&rdquo; would mean rejecting, and there is nothing in the text to suggest that Gilliam rejected his quilt-like paintings. Instead, the text indicates that he was exploring and pursuing a new art style in these paintings. Choice C is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;hiding&rdquo; would mean concealing from view, and there is nothing in the text to suggest that Gilliam attempted to conceal his quilt-like paintings. Instead, the text indicates that he was exploring and pursuing a new art style in these paintings.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-056-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"6f7e8e5b-d3a7-47cb-afdf-42f6736727a5","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959324,"updateDate":1691007959324}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959324, 1691007959324),
    ($SATQ$5fe53347$SATQ$, $SATQ$e10a23f1-8979-408d-8e57-c4ab2a0fac84$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Botanist Al Kovaleski has pointed out that maple trees already thrive in a wide variety of climates and thus may <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> changes in climate better than some other tree species do. The alterations maples may undergo in response to a changing climate are likely to be relatively small and easily achieved.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"20ef6cf9-8829-46b6-b364-533b5cd8a2cf","content":"<p>relocate from</p>"},{"id":"c8e75764-dffe-4272-9aa3-a59f9c4e4ce9","content":"<p>refer to</p>"},{"id":"fa0e47ed-efef-4c64-aad2-36ffb87f62a1","content":"<p>originate from</p>"},{"id":"59b48a0b-75d6-4e54-9fff-d0eaabaed110","content":"<p>adapt to</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["59b48a0b-75d6-4e54-9fff-d0eaabaed110"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of how maple trees may respond to changes in climate. As used in this context, "adapt to" means to adjust to new conditions. The text first notes that maple trees already thrive in a wide variety of climates and then goes on to say that alterations maples may undergo are likely to be relatively small and easily achieved. This context supports the idea that maple trees will more easily adjust or adapt to a changing climate.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text discusses how maple trees adjust to change, the trees do not "relocate from," or physically move from one place to another. Choice B is incorrect because "refer to" means to mention or allude to something, and the trees are not doing either of these actions. Choice C is incorrect because the text is discussing how maple trees may respond to changes in climate, not where they "originate from," or came from originally.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5fe53347","external_id":"e10a23f1-8979-408d-8e57-c4ab2a0fac84","vaultid":null,"uId":"00bea48d-89fd-4432-87e4-bda5b30c3568","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Botanist Al Kovaleski has pointed out that maple trees already thrive in a wide variety of climates and thus may <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> changes in climate better than some other tree species do. The alterations maples may undergo in response to a changing climate are likely to be relatively small and easily achieved.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"20ef6cf9-8829-46b6-b364-533b5cd8a2cf","content":"<p>relocate from</p>"},{"id":"c8e75764-dffe-4272-9aa3-a59f9c4e4ce9","content":"<p>refer to</p>"},{"id":"fa0e47ed-efef-4c64-aad2-36ffb87f62a1","content":"<p>originate from</p>"},{"id":"59b48a0b-75d6-4e54-9fff-d0eaabaed110","content":"<p>adapt to</p>"}],"keys":["59b48a0b-75d6-4e54-9fff-d0eaabaed110"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of how maple trees may respond to changes in climate. As used in this context, \"adapt to\" means to adjust to new conditions. The text first notes that maple trees already thrive in a wide variety of climates and then goes on to say that alterations maples may undergo are likely to be relatively small and easily achieved. This context supports the idea that maple trees will more easily adjust or adapt to a changing climate.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text discusses how maple trees adjust to change, the trees do not \"relocate from,\" or physically move from one place to another. Choice B is incorrect because \"refer to\" means to mention or allude to something, and the trees are not doing either of these actions. Choice C is incorrect because the text is discussing how maple trees may respond to changes in climate, not where they \"originate from,\" or came from originally.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669214,"updateDate":1743430669214}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669214, 1743430669214),
    ($SATQ$6017ae31$SATQ$, $SATQ$7649fd6d-86c7-4093-a334-634230e72c1d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 2016, Gabriela Gonz&aacute;lez and team announced that a chirping sound captured by Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory antennas was direct evidence of gravitational waves, which skeptics had argued would be too faint for detection. Detailed statistical analysis helped preclude claims of the event&rsquo;s <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>, confirming the signal at a confidence level of over 99%.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"abbbaebf-8a95-44b4-bd4f-61bc6d4a250a","content":"<p>inconspicuousness</p>"},{"id":"023ac1eb-39ba-404c-8a45-a68e6e20c397","content":"<p>discretion</p>"},{"id":"7dc5852b-784e-4c83-a3cd-f6e9b8c0491e","content":"<p>ambiguity</p>"},{"id":"dae5bdd7-c45f-4951-874a-89bbcc7a4326","content":"<p>probability</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7dc5852b-784e-4c83-a3cd-f6e9b8c0491e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Gabriela Gonz&aacute;lez and team&rsquo;s detection of gravitational waves. In this context, "ambiguity" means uncertainty or doubtfulness. The text explains that although skeptics had thought that direct evidence of gravitational waves would be too faint to be detected, researchers led by Gonz&aacute;lez claimed that a chirping sound captured by Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory antennas nevertheless provided such evidence. The text goes on to say that detailed statistical analysis confirmed the observation of gravitational waves with a high degree of confidence&mdash;that is, with near certainty&mdash;a finding that helped to preclude, or rule out, any claims that the signal&rsquo;s attribution to gravitational waves might be ambiguous or doubtful.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In this context, "inconspicuousness" would mean the quality of being unnoticeable or difficult to detect. Although the text indicates that skeptics had doubted whether gravitational waves could be observed directly because of their presumed faintness (which suggests that gravitational waves were expected to be difficult to detect), the blank portion of the text isn&rsquo;t referring to the possibility that gravitational waves are unnoticeable or undetectable. Instead, the focus of the last sentence is Gonz&aacute;lez&rsquo;s team&rsquo;s observation of a chirping sound that they attributed to gravitational waves, and it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that through statistical analysis, they ruled out the possibility that the sound they observed was undetectable. Rather, the skeptical view presented in the text suggests that there could be some ambiguity about the source of the chirping, but statistical analysis virtually eliminated this uncertainty. Choice B is incorrect because in this context, "discretion" would mean good judgment, and it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that an event, such as the detection of gravitational waves, would show judgment, much less that the event&rsquo;s capacity to exercise good judgment would be precluded by statistical analysis confirming its attribution. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, "probability" would mean likelihood, and the text states that statistical analysis, which confirmed the signal with a high degree of confidence, suggests the likelihood that the chirping sound was produced by gravitational waves, not that the analysis helped to preclude this likelihood.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6017ae31","external_id":"7649fd6d-86c7-4093-a334-634230e72c1d","vaultid":null,"uId":"f0797813-27e9-48ef-bd08-4dc5c84892c7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 2016, Gabriela Gonz&aacute;lez and team announced that a chirping sound captured by Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory antennas was direct evidence of gravitational waves, which skeptics had argued would be too faint for detection. Detailed statistical analysis helped preclude claims of the event&rsquo;s <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>, confirming the signal at a confidence level of over 99%.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"abbbaebf-8a95-44b4-bd4f-61bc6d4a250a","content":"<p>inconspicuousness</p>"},{"id":"023ac1eb-39ba-404c-8a45-a68e6e20c397","content":"<p>discretion</p>"},{"id":"7dc5852b-784e-4c83-a3cd-f6e9b8c0491e","content":"<p>ambiguity</p>"},{"id":"dae5bdd7-c45f-4951-874a-89bbcc7a4326","content":"<p>probability</p>"}],"keys":["7dc5852b-784e-4c83-a3cd-f6e9b8c0491e"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Gabriela Gonz&aacute;lez and team&rsquo;s detection of gravitational waves. In this context, \"ambiguity\" means uncertainty or doubtfulness. The text explains that although skeptics had thought that direct evidence of gravitational waves would be too faint to be detected, researchers led by Gonz&aacute;lez claimed that a chirping sound captured by Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory antennas nevertheless provided such evidence. The text goes on to say that detailed statistical analysis confirmed the observation of gravitational waves with a high degree of confidence&mdash;that is, with near certainty&mdash;a finding that helped to preclude, or rule out, any claims that the signal&rsquo;s attribution to gravitational waves might be ambiguous or doubtful.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In this context, \"inconspicuousness\" would mean the quality of being unnoticeable or difficult to detect. Although the text indicates that skeptics had doubted whether gravitational waves could be observed directly because of their presumed faintness (which suggests that gravitational waves were expected to be difficult to detect), the blank portion of the text isn&rsquo;t referring to the possibility that gravitational waves are unnoticeable or undetectable. Instead, the focus of the last sentence is Gonz&aacute;lez&rsquo;s team&rsquo;s observation of a chirping sound that they attributed to gravitational waves, and it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that through statistical analysis, they ruled out the possibility that the sound they observed was undetectable. Rather, the skeptical view presented in the text suggests that there could be some ambiguity about the source of the chirping, but statistical analysis virtually eliminated this uncertainty. Choice B is incorrect because in this context, \"discretion\" would mean good judgment, and it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that an event, such as the detection of gravitational waves, would show judgment, much less that the event&rsquo;s capacity to exercise good judgment would be precluded by statistical analysis confirming its attribution. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, \"probability\" would mean likelihood, and the text states that statistical analysis, which confirmed the signal with a high degree of confidence, suggests the likelihood that the chirping sound was produced by gravitational waves, not that the analysis helped to preclude this likelihood.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669208,"updateDate":1743430669208}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669208, 1743430669208),
    ($SATQ$617a8a10$SATQ$, $SATQ$99796d7a-46f5-4858-8eb8-9ee4c9d9f526$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Very little is known about the role nocturnal insects, such as moths, play in flower pollination because it is difficult to monitor insects at night. <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">To address this problem, a team of scientists used time-lapse cameras to record pollinator visits to red clover all day and night.</span> The recordings showed that while most pollinator visits were by bumblebees, one-third of visits were by moths. Additionally, flowers that were visited by both moths and bees produced more seeds than flowers that were only visited by bees.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the function of the underlined sentence?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"036b1c29-71fb-4167-896a-69c6941ab9e9","content":"<p>To describe an approach a team of scientists used to study pollinators&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"1166d08d-e3a6-40ff-9e6e-7e3e7daa967a","content":"<p>To question a claim scientists make about pollinators&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"bdcee8f0-cdc6-448e-939a-fa9b25c89ec7","content":"<p>To explain why moths prefer red clover to other flowers</p>"},{"id":"ec27e1a5-bfc8-4477-be55-8adb304b3534","content":"<p>To announce an unexpected research finding about red clover&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["036b1c29-71fb-4167-896a-69c6941ab9e9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes the function of the underlined sentence. The sentence indicates that scientists used time-lapse photography during both day and night to detect pollinators visiting red clover. Therefore, the underlined sentence has the function of describing an approach scientists used in the pollinator study discussed in the text.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the underlined sentence discusses equipment the scientists used to conduct their study, and the text provides nothing to suggest these elements of their experimental design could, by themselves, question claims resulting from the study. Choice C is incorrect because nothing in either the underlined sentence or the rest of the text addresses whether moths have a preference for red clover or any other flowers. Choice D is incorrect. Although the sentences that follow the underlined sentence discuss research findings, nothing in the text suggests that these findings were unexpected. Moreover, the underlined sentence describes part of the scientists&rsquo; experimental design (day and night time-lapse photography), not a finding of the scientists&rsquo; study.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"617a8a10","external_id":"99796d7a-46f5-4858-8eb8-9ee4c9d9f526","vaultid":null,"uId":"2abccfa3-61cd-42dd-9d4a-1d073b749b79","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Very little is known about the role nocturnal insects, such as moths, play in flower pollination because it is difficult to monitor insects at night. <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">To address this problem, a team of scientists used time-lapse cameras to record pollinator visits to red clover all day and night.</span> The recordings showed that while most pollinator visits were by bumblebees, one-third of visits were by moths. Additionally, flowers that were visited by both moths and bees produced more seeds than flowers that were only visited by bees.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the function of the underlined sentence?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"036b1c29-71fb-4167-896a-69c6941ab9e9","content":"<p>To describe an approach a team of scientists used to study pollinators&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"1166d08d-e3a6-40ff-9e6e-7e3e7daa967a","content":"<p>To question a claim scientists make about pollinators&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"bdcee8f0-cdc6-448e-939a-fa9b25c89ec7","content":"<p>To explain why moths prefer red clover to other flowers</p>"},{"id":"ec27e1a5-bfc8-4477-be55-8adb304b3534","content":"<p>To announce an unexpected research finding about red clover&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["036b1c29-71fb-4167-896a-69c6941ab9e9"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes the function of the underlined sentence. The sentence indicates that scientists used time-lapse photography during both day and night to detect pollinators visiting red clover. Therefore, the underlined sentence has the function of describing an approach scientists used in the pollinator study discussed in the text.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the underlined sentence discusses equipment the scientists used to conduct their study, and the text provides nothing to suggest these elements of their experimental design could, by themselves, question claims resulting from the study. Choice C is incorrect because nothing in either the underlined sentence or the rest of the text addresses whether moths have a preference for red clover or any other flowers. Choice D is incorrect. Although the sentences that follow the underlined sentence discuss research findings, nothing in the text suggests that these findings were unexpected. Moreover, the underlined sentence describes part of the scientists&rsquo; experimental design (day and night time-lapse photography), not a finding of the scientists&rsquo; study.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323041,"updateDate":1730147323041}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323041, 1730147323041),
    ($SATQ$6286dd96$SATQ$, $SATQ$b867f47a-68a4-4c84-a661-e10ad53f6c12$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Frances Hodgson Burnett&rsquo;s 1905 novel <em>A Little Princess</em>. Sara is a young student at a school in London.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Sara not only could tell stories, but she adored telling them. When she sat or stood in the midst of a circle and began to <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>invent</u></span> wonderful things, her green eyes grew big and shining, her cheeks flushed, and, without knowing that she was doing it, she began to act and made what she told lovely or alarming by the raising or dropping of her voice.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;invent&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e2c9cbc8-fb10-41fe-8a1b-d4ae027db03a","content":"<p>Mislead</p>"},{"id":"de063c02-7fe2-432f-952a-6092ffc41650","content":"<p>Disguise</p>"},{"id":"f323a61c-637c-49c7-913c-9a5c007bb857","content":"<p>Create</p>"},{"id":"cecfa0fc-f146-4358-af9f-d48c172ded2d","content":"<p>Discover</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f323a61c-637c-49c7-913c-9a5c007bb857"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because as used in the text, "invent" most nearly means create or make up. The text begins by stating that Sara likes to tell stories and thoroughly enjoys telling them. The rest of the text describes how she looks and acts when she invents, or creates, "wonderful things" in her stories ("what she told").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text suggests that Sara makes up "wonderful things," nothing suggests that she does so to mislead, or intentionally deceive, others; there is no reason to assume that Sara&rsquo;s listeners don&rsquo;t know that she&rsquo;s creating the stories she shares. Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that Sara is disguising, or trying to cover up or conceal, "wonderful things" when she tells stories; rather, she is creating them and presenting them in ways that make them "lovely or alarming" for her listeners. Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that Sara is discovering, or unexpectedly finding, "wonderful things" in the stories she loves to tell; rather, that she is making things up as she tells the stories.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6286dd96","external_id":"b867f47a-68a4-4c84-a661-e10ad53f6c12","vaultid":null,"uId":"66365032-701e-4d97-b8e1-37972dbf67e7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Frances Hodgson Burnett&rsquo;s 1905 novel <em>A Little Princess</em>. Sara is a young student at a school in London.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Sara not only could tell stories, but she adored telling them. When she sat or stood in the midst of a circle and began to <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>invent</u></span> wonderful things, her green eyes grew big and shining, her cheeks flushed, and, without knowing that she was doing it, she began to act and made what she told lovely or alarming by the raising or dropping of her voice.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;invent&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e2c9cbc8-fb10-41fe-8a1b-d4ae027db03a","content":"<p>Mislead</p>"},{"id":"de063c02-7fe2-432f-952a-6092ffc41650","content":"<p>Disguise</p>"},{"id":"f323a61c-637c-49c7-913c-9a5c007bb857","content":"<p>Create</p>"},{"id":"cecfa0fc-f146-4358-af9f-d48c172ded2d","content":"<p>Discover</p>"}],"keys":["f323a61c-637c-49c7-913c-9a5c007bb857"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because as used in the text, \"invent\" most nearly means create or make up. The text begins by stating that Sara likes to tell stories and thoroughly enjoys telling them. The rest of the text describes how she looks and acts when she invents, or creates, \"wonderful things\" in her stories (\"what she told\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text suggests that Sara makes up \"wonderful things,\" nothing suggests that she does so to mislead, or intentionally deceive, others; there is no reason to assume that Sara&rsquo;s listeners don&rsquo;t know that she&rsquo;s creating the stories she shares. Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that Sara is disguising, or trying to cover up or conceal, \"wonderful things\" when she tells stories; rather, she is creating them and presenting them in ways that make them \"lovely or alarming\" for her listeners. Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that Sara is discovering, or unexpectedly finding, \"wonderful things\" in the stories she loves to tell; rather, that she is making things up as she tells the stories.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323032,"updateDate":1730147323032}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323032, 1730147323032),
    ($SATQ$62a18353$SATQ$, $SATQ$57367f5a-845b-41c3-a3e2-6150dc98d690$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$37fbc28a-447b-4a2c-ae33-b6fd0dc309a7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Zora Neale Hurston&rsquo;s 1921 short story &ldquo;John Redding Goes to Sea.&rdquo; John wants to travel far beyond the village where he lives near his mother, Matty.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[John] had on several occasions attempted to <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>reconcile his mother to</u></span> the notion, but found it a difficult task. Matty always took refuge in self-pity and tears. Her son&rsquo;s desires were incomprehensible to her, that was all.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the phrase &ldquo;reconcile his mother to&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"89233e02-05d7-49aa-baa1-ec5e9ac21eb1","content":"<p>Get his mother to accept</p>"},{"id":"d569a3d3-f429-4b40-9bad-9ca2dd87b90a","content":"<p>Get his mother to apologize for</p>"},{"id":"7331484c-6995-44e6-91a4-e3e4ffaa5864","content":"<p>Get his mother to match</p>"},{"id":"85ab33a2-a8f6-4f0f-90cd-a1b18076e99f","content":"<p>Get his mother to reunite with</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["89233e02-05d7-49aa-baa1-ec5e9ac21eb1"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The expression &ldquo;reconcile to&rdquo; means &ldquo;to cause (a person) to accept something difficult or disagreeable.&rdquo; The text suggests that John wants his mother to accept his desire to travel, even though she doesn&rsquo;t like that idea. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t make sense. John doesn&rsquo;t want his mother to apologize for his own desire to travel&mdash;he wants her to accept his desire to travel. Choice C is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that John wants his mother to match his desire to travel. Rather, he wants her to accept his desire to travel even though she doesn&rsquo;t like it. Choice D is incorrect. This is tempting, because it seems to pick up on the idea of people &ldquo;reconciling&rdquo; after a fight, but it actually doesn&rsquo;t make sense. The text never suggests that John&rsquo;s mother was &ldquo;united with&rdquo; the idea of him traveling in the past&mdash;if anything, it seems like she&rsquo;s always been against it. Besides, it would be strange to say that a person &ldquo;reunites with&rdquo; a notion. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-H001$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$1773509d-64eb-4340-98a0-c9e401ae68b4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"62a18353","external_id":"57367f5a-845b-41c3-a3e2-6150dc98d690","vaultid":"37fbc28a-447b-4a2c-ae33-b6fd0dc309a7","uId":"b984dc01-5669-43be-8069-6d31b2f8194e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Zora Neale Hurston&rsquo;s 1921 short story &ldquo;John Redding Goes to Sea.&rdquo; John wants to travel far beyond the village where he lives near his mother, Matty.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>[John] had on several occasions attempted to <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>reconcile his mother to</u></span> the notion, but found it a difficult task. Matty always took refuge in self-pity and tears. Her son&rsquo;s desires were incomprehensible to her, that was all.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the phrase &ldquo;reconcile his mother to&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"89233e02-05d7-49aa-baa1-ec5e9ac21eb1","content":"<p>Get his mother to accept</p>"},{"id":"d569a3d3-f429-4b40-9bad-9ca2dd87b90a","content":"<p>Get his mother to apologize for</p>"},{"id":"7331484c-6995-44e6-91a4-e3e4ffaa5864","content":"<p>Get his mother to match</p>"},{"id":"85ab33a2-a8f6-4f0f-90cd-a1b18076e99f","content":"<p>Get his mother to reunite with</p>"}],"keys":["89233e02-05d7-49aa-baa1-ec5e9ac21eb1"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The expression &ldquo;reconcile to&rdquo; means &ldquo;to cause (a person) to accept something difficult or disagreeable.&rdquo; The text suggests that John wants his mother to accept his desire to travel, even though she doesn&rsquo;t like that idea. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t make sense. John doesn&rsquo;t want his mother to apologize for his own desire to travel&mdash;he wants her to accept his desire to travel. Choice C is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that John wants his mother to match his desire to travel. Rather, he wants her to accept his desire to travel even though she doesn&rsquo;t like it. Choice D is incorrect. This is tempting, because it seems to pick up on the idea of people &ldquo;reconciling&rdquo; after a fight, but it actually doesn&rsquo;t make sense. The text never suggests that John&rsquo;s mother was &ldquo;united with&rdquo; the idea of him traveling in the past&mdash;if anything, it seems like she&rsquo;s always been against it. Besides, it would be strange to say that a person &ldquo;reunites with&rdquo; a notion. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-H001","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"1773509d-64eb-4340-98a0-c9e401ae68b4","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377)
